\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Renew Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 1,738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2003\par 06:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Tax Plan Aids Shares\par DJIA          8594.02    gain 77.59     up 0.9%\par NASDAQ        1507.55    gain 17.68     up 1.2%\par S&P 500       931.87     gain 8.45      up 0.9%\par Dow Future    8593.00    gain 16.00     up 0.2%\par NASDAQ Future 1133.50    gain 3.50      up 0.3%\par S&P Future    933.00     gain 2.25      up 0.2%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.31% dn 0.08\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Wall Street regained some upward momentum Thursday, rising on news that President Bush would sign a tax cut bill into law and on a favorable court decision for cigarette makers including Altria. Treasurys continued their torrid rally. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Equities gained after Bush announced he would sign a dramatically reduced, compromise version of his tax cut plan. The plan calls for $350 billion in tax cuts, just under half the $726 billion package Bush wanted. \par \par \uc2  "The tax bill should excite people...I think a lot of people said that it wouldn't make it. That generates some enthusiasm," said Ned Riley, chief investment strategist at State Street Global Advisors in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Aside from the tax cut plan, analysts also attributed Thursday's buying to better-than-expected earnings and the fact that the market was breaking important psychological levels with the Nasdaq rising above the 1,500 level. \par \par \uc2  "That is the top that we saw in November of last year. The fact that we have seen the potential for this resistance level turning to support is going to get (investors) more excited and keep them in the market," said Chris Johnson, manager of quantitative analysis at Schaeffer's Investment Research in Cincinnati. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: Treasury prices remained in a bullish mood Thursday, led by intermediate and long-dated maturities. \par \par \uc2  The long bond's yield was up over a full point and trading at a record low of 4.29%, with the demand for longer-dated Treasurys due to the market focusing anew on recent comments by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  In his testimony to Congress Wednesday, the Fed chief declared that the fight against inflation was over, with the risk of deflation outweighing the prospect of rising prices in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "By declaring that the fight against inflation has been won Greenspan is telling the market that rates will remain low for some time," said Richard Gilhooly, senior fixed income strategist at BNP paribas in New York. He said that a long bond yield of 4% in the near term was not out of the question given that insurance companies and pension funds have effectively been told by Greenspan to hedge against the risk of deflation. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets are also fixated on the Fed's response to further economic weakness and falling prices. Jim Cusser, portfolio manager at Waddell & Reed in Kansas City, Mo., noted that the market is anticipating the prospect of the Fed buying Treasurys to boost the economy. He said the current rally in Treasurys "is a function of an economy that, despite all the monetary and fiscal stimulus we have seen and expect, shows no sign of lifting sharply." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo, Other Markets Follow Wall Street Higher\par USD-Yen             117.35       gain  0.16    up 0.2%\par AUD-USD             0.6558       loss  0.0024  dn 0.4%\par Nikkei 225          8188.77      gain  137511  up 1.7%\par Hang Seng           9275.08      gain  143.59  up 1.6%\par JGB Yield           0.5550% down 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks rallied Friday morning on broad gains\par by technology issues. The dollar was lower against the Japanese yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo took its cue from an upswing in the U.S. market Thursday that\par pushed the tech-heavy Nasdaq composite index above the psychologically\par important 1,500 level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Local high-tech shares Sony, NEC and Canon led the advance in the\par morning session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese business activity grew slightly in the January-March quarter,\par underscoring that Japan's economy is idling and keeping the heat on the\par government and the Bank of Japan to do more to spark a recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other markets in the region also rallied.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets May Close Week On Cheery Note\par Euro-USD            1.1675      loss  0.0025  dn 0.2%\par Stlg-USD            1.6324      loss  0.0026  dn 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.2981      gain  0.0037  up 0.3%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares, bond prices and the euro are set\par to start the session higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to open higher, especially\par technology shares. But with U.S. and U.K. holidays on Monday, investors\par might elect to take some late profit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks crept higher Thursday as the financial sector was\par buoyed by a raft of relatively resilient earnings reports.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Robert Parkes, strategist at HSBC, said markets seem to have reached a\par plateau. "The risks to the macroeconomic outlook remain on the downside\par and even if there is a recovery, it will be slow and gradual," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The speed of the dollar's depreciation over recent weeks is likely to\par exact a toll on European corporate balance sheets during the\par second-quarter results season, Parkes said. "With the terrorist threat\par also reappearing on investors' radar screens, we expect European markets\par to trade flat for the rest of the year," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are expected to rise after\par after the yield curve continued to flatten as deflation talk pushed long\par yields lower on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is biased after (U.S. Federal Reserve Board Chairman) Alan\par Greenspan's comments. It is still looking good, but it's driven mainly\par by technicals," a dealer in London said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The flattening move started several weeks ago, as people started\par anticipating deflation, and Greenspan's comments have triggered an\par overreaction on the market," said Rene Defossez, strategist at CDC Ixis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said fair value for the two-year/10-year spread would be 30 basis\par points wider than the current levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is little risk of deflation in Europe as far as there is no\par talk of wage decreases, and it is very hard to believe that in Germany,\par for example, unions would accept any talk on this issue," Defossez said.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session in a slightly higher range just\par under $1.1700.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar traded steady to weaker in narrow ranges Thursday after an\par anticlimactic U.S. jobs report failed to jolt much life into a market\par already winding down in anticipation of next week's holidays in the U.S.\par and the U.K.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Compared with day-earlier levels, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was flat against\par the yen and sterling but down modestly against the euro and Swiss franc,\par said traders.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Ahead of the long weekend, the market had pretty much finished by the\par time New York came in," said Tom Molloy, a trader with Bank Leumi in New\par York. "We just appear to be marking time between now and Tuesday as\par liquidity is already thinning."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A decisive breach of $1.1750, roughly the level at which the euro\par began trading in 1999, could open the road for an assault on the single\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's historical high at $1.1888 and points above. In Thursday's\par trading, the euro managed to climb as far as $1.1728 after falling back\par from the new four-year high of $1.1744 reached Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moreover, the strong hand of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par still in play, supporting the dollar above Y116.50 in the near term,\par analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market didn't really react to figures released Thursday by the\par U.S. Labor Department indicating initial jobless claims rose for the\par first time in a month by a larger-than-expected 7,000 to 428,000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Friday, May 23: French, German Inflation, Issing\par ET/GMT\par 0100/0500 Japan        April 7-8 Bank Of Japan meeting minutes\par 0130/0530 Japan        April Nationwide Department Store Sales\par 0245/0645 France       April Consumer Spending (seen -0.4% MM)\par 0250/0650 France       Final April Consumer Price Index\par 0300/0700 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Member Issing\par 0430/0830 U.K.         1Q GDP (seen +0.2% QQ, +2.3% YY)\par 1300/1700 U.S.         Nymex closes\par 1400/1800 U.S.         Bond markets close\par   N/A     Germany      Preliminary May Consumer Price Index\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wolverhampton & Dudley (U.WOL): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit before exceptionals (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP29.1M\par (GBP31.3M)\par Note:Analysts looking for update on performance of pub business and\par will likely query company on its interest in Eldridge Pope which is\par rumored to be for sale.\par Office Depot (ODP), Guilbert : E.U. antitrust decision\par Note: Retailer Pinault-Printemps-Redoute has sealed a final accord with\par Office Depot on the EUR815M sale of its Guilbert office products\par division. Under terms of the deal, sealed only four days after the\par pair entered a 30-day exclusive talks period, Office Depot will pay\par PPR a further EUR40M if its share price exceeds $20 for five\par consecutive trading session in the 18 months that follow the expected\par approval by E.U. competition authorities.\par SEB (F.SEB), Moulinex : E.U. antitrust decision\par Note: Seb acquired the Moulinex and Krups brands, some design bureaus\par and marketing units, as well as seven production sites in France and\par overseas late last year, after the Moulinex company was placed in\par receivership. The European Union Commission has given conditional\par approval to the tie-up, requesting some concessions be made in nine\par European countries, where Seb will lose its rights on the Moulinex\par brand.\par Castings (U.CSG): FY Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (Multex, 4 analysts): GBP9M (GBP9.9M)\par Forecast range: GBP8.8M - GBP9.3M\par Consensus sales: GBP56.3M (GBP56M)\par BBAG (R.BBO): 1Q Earnings\par Euronext (N.ENX): 1Q Earnings\par Note: Analysts watching for signs of rising operating costs and\par possible impact of weaker sterling on earnings. Merrill Lynch sees\par Euronext reporting 1Q EBITA of EUR65.9M, net profit of EUR28.1M. KBC\par Securities reckons EBITA at EUR66.1M and net at EUR34.5M. Euronext did\par not publish separate quarterly figures last year.\par Scottish Radio Holdings (U.RAD): 1H Earnings\par Continental (G.CGU): AGM\par Enel (EN): AGM\par 2 Travel (U.TLG): EGM\par A