\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar feels the heat again, euro eyes launch rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2003\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  By Burton Frierson LONDON, May 22 (Reuters) - The dollar came under pressure again on Thursday as markets used the euro's overnight dip to buy into further gains and drive it back towards its 1999 launch rate. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit fresh four-year highs on Wednesday but later eased after failing to break its $1.1747 launch rate and U.S. Federal Reserve Chief Alan Greenspan soothed markets with moderately optimistic comments about the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro's fall provided good levels to buy back. But they said the market needed a big push before breaking through the launch rate, knowing it would set off stop loss orders that could vault the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even higher. \par \par \uc2  "We did always expect it to be difficult to break through the birth rate, and we were never going to do it in one move. We've tried it three times now - it's a big psychological level, like parity," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  By 1145 GMT, the euro had pushed to the day's highs of $1.1715, up over two thirds of a percent up from its New York close but still short of Wednesday's four-year peak at $1.1745. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded down a third of a percent on the yen, having fallen as low as 117.03 yen after earlier hitting a two-week high in Asian trade. It hit a two-year low of 115.10 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was just firmer at 137.15 yen having hit a record high of 137.40 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  POLICYMAKERS IN FOCUS Earlier the euro came under mild pressure on worries the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might unexpectedly cut interest rates at its regular mid-monthly meeting. The ECB does not discuss monetary policy as a rule at mid-month meetings but reserves the right to put it on the agenda if it wants. \par \par \uc2  Analysts doubted the ECB would cut interest rates, now at 2.50 percent, on Thursday but some forecast a reduction at its June meeting after recent poor German growth data. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it would take an aggressive campaign of ECB rate cuts to stymie the euro's rally and suspected options placed around the euro's launch level were currently helping to hold it back. \par \par \uc2  "They do exist at all seemingly important levels and the euro launch level is a seemingly important level," said Mark Austin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets. \par \par \uc2  "So I'm sure that it is true that there are some barriers there which price action in the options market will be there to defend." \par \par \uc2  The first May inflation data out of Germany showed a 0.2 percent monthly fall in consumer prices in the state of Hesse, boosting expectations the ECB will cut interest rates in June. \par \par \uc2  Finland's representative at the ECB said the strong euro was hitting French, German and Finnish exports. \par \par \uc2  Matti Louekoski, deputising for Finnish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Matti Vanhala, told reporters: "(The euro) means problems because of our big dependence on exports." \par \par \uc2  In Asia, dealers suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during New York trade on Wednesday to squash any signs of gains in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and some said it may have stepped in on Thursday too. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are worried about the effect of a strong yen on exports and are widely seen as wanting to prevent the dollar from dropping through 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  In testimony before the U.S. Congress on Wednesday Greenspan gave few clues on monetary policy but called deflation a "minor threat" and held out hopes the U.S. economy would pick up. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030522dz5m00nry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
