\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar back under pressure, euro eyes birth rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2003\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  By Burton Frierson LONDON, May 22 (Reuters) - The dollar gave up Thursday's early rally on the euro as markets used the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's overnight dip to buy into further gains and possibly mount another attempt to break through its 1999 launch rate. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit fresh four-year highs on Wednesday but later eased after failing to break its $1.1747 launch rate and U.S. Federal Reserve Chief Alan Greenspan soothed markets with optimistic comments about the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro's move down provided good levels to buy back. But they said the market needed a big push before breaking through the launch rate, knowing it would set off stop loss orders that could vault the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even higher. \par \par \uc2  "We did always expect it to be difficult to break through the birth rate, and we were never going to do it in one move. We've tried it three times now - it's a big psychological level, like parity," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  By 0925 GMT, the euro had pushed to the day's highs of $1.1700, two thirds of a percent up from its New York close but still short of Wednesday's four-year peak at $1.1745. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down almost half a percent at 117.03 yen after hitting a two-week high in Asian trade but was still away from Monday's two-year low of 115.10. \par \par \uc2  The euro was a touch firmer at 137.18 yen having hit a record high of 137.40 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  POLICYMAKERS IN FOCUS Earlier the euro came under mild pressure on worries the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might unexpectedly cut interest rates at its regular mid-monthly meeting. The ECB does not discuss monetary policy as a rule at mid-month meetings but reserves the right to put it on the agenda if it wants. \par \par \uc2  Analysts doubted the ECB would cut interest rates, now at 2.50 percent, on Thursday but some were forecasting a reduction at its June meeting after recent poor German growth data. \par \par \uc2  "If the ECB becomes aggressive it has potential to cut this euro rally but a 50 basis point cut expected in June won't be enough," said Jim McCormick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  The first May inflation data out of Germany on Thursday showed a 0.2 percent monthly fall in consumer prices in the state of Hesse, boosting expectations the ECB will cut interest rates in June. \par \par \uc2  Finland's representative at the ECB said the strong euro was hitting French German and Finnish exports. \par \par \uc2  Matti Louekoski, deputising for Finnish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Matti Vanhala, told reporters: "(The euro) means problems because of our big dependence on exports." \par \par \uc2  In Asia, dealers suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during New York trade on Wednesday to squash any signs of gains in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and some said it may have stepped in on Thursday too. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are worried about the effect of a strong yen on exports and are widely seen as wanting to prevent the dollar from dropping through 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  In testimony before the U.S. Congress on Wednesday Greenspan gave few clues on monetary policy but called deflation a "minor threat" and held out hopes the U.S. economy would pick up. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030522dz5m00m2l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
