\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar claws back on Greenspan, BOJ caution. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2003\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 22 (Reuters) - The dollar regained some ground against the yen and the euro on Thursday after optimistic comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan shattered expectations of an imminent interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Caution over possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also kept speculators from selling dollars for yen, keeping the greenback confined within a tight range of 117.40-117.79 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers suspect that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during New York trading hours on Wednesday, and some said it may have stepped in on Thursday as well. \par \par \uc2  "You cannot expect much to happen during Tokyo time as strong wariness over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is restraining activity here," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is in a corrective mode ahead of the long weekend, while the dollar is being bought back after comments from Greenspan, but basically weak sentiment for the dollar still persists," he said. \par \par \uc2  Both New York and London markets will be closed on Monday for holidays. \par \par \uc2  In testimony before Congress on Wednesday, Greenspan gave few clues on monetary policy but called deflation a "minor threat" and held out hopes that the U.S. economy would pick up. \par \par \uc2  "Expectations for a rate cut prior to the next FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee) meeting in June have eased after Greenspan's comments yesterday, but the market is still anticipating a credit easing at the meeting," said a spot trader at a European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  At 0546 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 117.59/64 yen against Wednesday's late U.S. level of 117.47/57 yen. \par \par \uc2  It hit a two-week high of 117.79 yen in Sydney trading hours. \par \par \uc2  The euro is in a slight corrective phase ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-setting meeting early in June, at which the ECB is expected to ease interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The ECB Governing Council is scheduled to meet later in the day, but no interest rate announcement is scheduled. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1640/41 against $1.1636/42 in late U.S. trade, having fallen back from a four-year peak of $1.1745 hit on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was little changed at 136.89/96 yen It hit a record high of 137.40 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also underpinned by the view that the Japanese authorities, worried about the effect of a strong yen on exports, were serious about keeping it in check through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Not many want to try an attack under 117.50 since that would be the same as challenging the authorities," said another trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Japan is widely seen as wanting to stop the dollar's fall below 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  In the morning, Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said the yen's fall against the dollar from the previous day was a "natural correction" of an earlier move and declined to comment on whether authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Trade data in the morning that showed growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports slowed in April had little impact on the market. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030522dz5m00cz7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
