\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Slides Against Euro, Holds Steady Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 1,095 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2003\par 01:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- The dollar traded steady to weaker in narrow ranges\par Thursday after an anticlimactic U.S. jobs report failed to jolt much\par life into a market already winding down in anticipation of next week's\par holidays in the U.S. and the U.K.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the dollar was trading at &#165;117.22, virtually unchanged from &#165;117.25 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1699, up slightly from $1.1677 late a day earlier. The dollar was also at 1.2949 Swiss francs, down from 1.2966 francs late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.6352, little changed from $1.6359. \par \par \uc2  Compared with day-earlier levels, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was flat against the yen and sterling but down modestly against the euro and franc, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Ahead of the long weekend, the market had pretty much finished by the time New York came in," said Tom Molloy, a trader with Bank Leumi in New York. "We just appear to be marking time between now and Tuesday, as liquidity is already thinning." \par \par \uc2  U.S. markets will be closed Monday for Memorial Day, while U.K. traders will have the same day off for a Bank Holiday. Activity in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets usually tapers off ahead of holidays that keep major trading centers such as New York or London closed. \par \par \uc2  Given a lack of fresh news, buying and selling has mainly centered on adjusting positions. Thin conditions are likely to cause choppy trading again Friday, but analysts doubt the market has enough momentum to support a significant move for any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A decisive breach of $1.1750, roughly the level at which the euro began trading in 1999, could open the road for an assault on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's historical high at $1.1888 and points above. In Thursday's trading, the euro managed to climb as far as $1.1728 after falling back from the new four-year high at $1.1744 reached Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the strong hand of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still in play, supporting the dollar above &#165;116.50 in the near term, analysts said. In recent months, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through orders to Japanese banks to keep the yen from appreciating to levels the Japanese government feels would endanger chances for an export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The market let largely pass without reaction figures released Thursday by the Labor Department indicating initial jobless claims rose for the first time in a month by a larger-than-expected 7,000 to 428,000 in the week that ended May 17. \par \par \uc2  However, the four-week moving average, which smoothes out weekly fluctuations, fell to a five-week low of 433,000. A Labor Department spokesman said the increase could be almost entirely attributable to tornadoes that destroyed an automobile plant in Oklahoma. \par \par \uc2  "It's one of those numbers that was a bit worse, but not dramatically so," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Traders and investors were treated to comments Thursday from a top Treasury Department official who warned against the use of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial markets, saying it is often difficult for officials to know what impact their comments may have on the markets. \par \par \uc2  "Going back over the last several administrations, we have learned that the less verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we have in markets of all types -- bonds, stocks, exchange -- the better we are," Treasury Undersecretary for Domestic Finance Peter Fisher said, answering questions before the Senate Banking Subcommittee on Economic Policy. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Fisher also declined to comment on the dollar's weakness. "We have a policy of having the Secretary of the Treasury be the spokesman on the dollar. It is really prudent and in our nation's best interest to have one spokesman on the exchange rate," Mr. Fisher said. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Fisher's comments didn't have a noticeable impact on trading, but were regarded as ironic by some analysts. Some of the dollar's recent softness has been ascribed to comments by Mr. Snow, Mr. Fisher's boss. Mr. Snow has acknowledged benefits to U.S. exporters from a weaker dollar and played down the dollar's decline against its counterparts, thereby creating confusion as to whether the U.S. still pursues a strong dollar policy. Both Mr. Snow and White House spokesman Ari Fleischer have publicly stated the U.S. dollar policy is unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, in London, Prime Minister Tony Blair said his cabinet is coming to a consensus on whether the United Kingdom. should adopt the euro as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mr. Blair ducked a question on whether cabinet members would be able to override the British treasury's assessment of whether it is in the U.K.'s interest to join. \par \par \uc2  The British treasury has been analyzing the economic impact of adopting the euro. It is conducting five economic tests, the results of which will be presented to Parliament June 9. If the tests are met, the U.K. will hold a binding referendum on whether to give up sterling in favor of the euro. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Canadian dollar tumbled sharply lower against the U.S. dollar on Thursday, suffering a massive decline as unexpectedly weak inflation data for April and new developments in the mad-cow-disease situation combined to pressure the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The sharp decline in the Canadian dollar is likely to be short lived however, and the longer-term upward trend is likely to reassert itself next week as normal trading flows resume Tuesday, analysts said. Late Thursday, the U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3753, compared with C$1.3490 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Farther afield, the Brazilian real maintained its strength against the dollar, which weakened late in the day to 2.982 reals from 3.00 reals on Wednesday. The real's climb for the second straight day was largely attributed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrating its anti-inflation resolve by keeping the benchmark Selic rate at 26.5%, despite vociferous demands from government and industry leaders to loosen monetary policy and stave off a potential recession. \par \par \uc2  --Deborah Lagomarsino in Washington and Don Curren in Toronto contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to Michael Deibert at michael.deibert@dowjones.com and Robert Flint at robert.flint@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-22-03 1921ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030522dz5m0073l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
