\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up,But Capped By BOJ Efforts To Contain Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2003\par 10:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar strengthened modestly against its U.S. counterpart in Asia Wednesday but its gains were capped by rumors that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the flagging U.S. dollar versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  At 0815 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7247, down from S$1.7317 late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.7225 and S$1.7252 intraday. \par \par \uc2  With a lack of domestic news to drive trading, the Singapore dollar largely tracked the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar, which is already under broad downward pressure from concerns over the health of the domestic economy and the widening current-account deficit, took another hit this week after the U.S. Treasury Department hinted it may be shifting away from its long-standing strong-dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar probed the Y116.60 support level several times during Wednesday's session but bounced back each time, suggesting the Bank of Japan was at work to keep a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at Y116.65, down slightly from Y116.71 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The fear of U.S. dollar purchases by the BOJ helped limit the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "With a lot of people shortish dollar/Sing and rumors that the Bank of Japan was buying dollars, nobody wanted to push the dollar too hard," a trader at an Asian bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  He said the U.S. dollar could fall through S$1.7200 support during European trading Wednesday, with the potential for S$1.7180-S$1.7190 to become the next support levels during Asian trading Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds rallied across the curve Wednesday following overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds fell two basis points to close at 0.72%, while the yield on five-year bonds fell one basis point to 1.29%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the seven-year bond yield fell two basis points to 1.62%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 10-year bonds, which traded as low as 1.78% in the morning session, closed at 1.80%, down four basis points from a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds fell to 2.19% in early trading before closing at 2.22% compared with 2.25% a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, offers also tracked local bond\par yields lower. The two-year swap offer was quoted at 0.88%, down one\par basis point, while the five-year offer was down one basis point at\par 1.42%. The 10-year offer closed two basis points lower at 2.11%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 124 basis points\par from 125 basis points late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Wednesday    Late Tuesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.4375% 0.5625%   0.4375% 0.5000%\par 3-Month           0.5000% 0.6250%   0.5000% 0.6250%\par 1-Year            0.5625% 0.6875%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4045;\par alan.yonan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Simon Harvey\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030521dz5l000ik\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
