\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar steady, supported by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2003\par 07:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied on Tuesday, held above 117 yen by the threat of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the market waited for further comments from U.S. officials on their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  As of 0526 GMT, the dollar was at 117.09 yen little changed from Monday's late U.S. level. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the dollar rebounded about two yen from a two-year low near 115 yen, helped by what traders thought was \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. "I think this proved their strong commitment to protect 115 yen," said Katsunori Kitakura, treasury manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have declined to comment on whether they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Tuesday that such action should be taken if there were unnatural moves in the market. \par \par \uc2  Traders said 115 yen was widely seen as a breakeven point for Japanese export industries and the BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  each time the dollar has fallen towards that level in the past two years. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quiet at $1.1664 slightly above a late New York level of $1.1645 but still nearly a cent below a four-year high of $1.1740 hit in London on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at 136.52 yen off a record high of 136.85 hit in New York on Monday, but little changed from 136.43 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The market showed little reaction to a decision by the Bank of Japan to ease its monetary policy by raising its target for banks' excess reserves - a move to ensure stability in the financial markets after the bail-out of the country's fifth-largest bank, Resona Holdings. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said it would raise the target for current account deposits that banks keep at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to around 27 to 30 trillion yen ($230.6-256.2 billion). The previous target level was 22 to 27 trillion yen. "I think a move in this direction was within the market's expectations," said Chuo Mitsui's Kitakura. "The focus is rather on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and (U.S. Treasury Secretary John) Snow's testimony." \par \par \uc2  Snow is scheduled to appear before a Senate subcommittee at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The market has been rapidly selling the dollar since Snow mentioned the benefits of a weak dollar for U.S. exports last week. \par \par \uc2  Many market players think the euro will test its January 1999 launch level of $1.1747 and possibly a record high of $1.1886, given the widespread belief that the U.S. administration is happy to see the dollar fall, despite an official "strong dollar" policy. ($1=117.09 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030521dz5k00fl5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
