\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar pauses for breath, BOJ shadow lends support. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2003\par 14:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied on Tuesday following a roller-coaster plunge to four-year lows against the euro in the previous session while the threat of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept it supported against the yen. \par \par \uc2  After Monday's two-yen rally from two-year lows, the dollar remained on edge amid talk that Japan's monetary authorities may have returned briefly to the market on Tuesday after aggressive covert action in the previous session to curb yen gains. \par \par \uc2  It hovered near the previous session's close on the yen at 117.23 in nervous trade with dealers reporting strong interest to buy dollars from Japanese banks often associated with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it dipped briefly below 117.00. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a couple of Japanese names on the bid, the dollar fell below 117 and they came in. They are determined," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan money market data released on Tuesday suggested that Japanese authorities, which did not confirm \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, may have spent up to one trillion yen ($8.53 billion) that day to stem the yen's unwelcome rise. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are concerned gains in the yen will stymie export growth, the only bright spot in the stagnating economy, and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  detected on Monday seemed the most aggressive action this year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit session highs at $1.1604 per euro, drifting in a range between $1.1610-70 throughout the European session. It stayed near the previous day's closing levels but above Monday's four-year lows of $1.1738. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR DOWNTREND INTACT \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Tuesday's pullback was only temporary and the dollar was likely to resume its downward spiral, possibly bringing the euro back to its 1999 launch level of $1.1747 soon. \par \par \uc2  "There is nervousness in the market that the euro may fall a few times before it breaks the launch level, just as it happened when euro/dollar went through parity," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has long been plagued by the vast U.S. current account deficit, market worries over Washington's spending plans and the paltry yields on dollar instruments. \par \par \uc2  Fresh selling could start as soon as Wednesday when Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan testifies in Congress, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "He could trigger more selling by discussing disinflationary risks," said McMahon. "With short end rates as low as now, the conventional way of addressing disinflationary problems is to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall." \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve had issued a warning about the risk of U.S. deflation earlier this month and markets are on their toes to see whether the chairman reiterates this concern. \par \par \uc2  Still earlier, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow is in focus, after his comments on the dollar at the weekend and an apparent lack of concern over the unit's fall from his fellow Group of Seven policy-makers triggered the latest selling. \par \par \uc2  Snow, who called the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves "fairly modest", testifies in Congress at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  In the euro zone, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member and Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke raised expectations the ECB may be positioning for lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  He told a newspaper he saw no sign of deflation in Germany or Europe and there was room for manoeuvre in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030520dz5k00opc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
