\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles upward vs euro, Japan in focus. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2003\par 12:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar fought back on Tuesday after suffering a deep fall to four-year lows against the euro in the previous session, as investors took profits and waited for signs whether the Bank of Japan would return to the market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's plunge on Monday lost momentum when Japan's monetary authorities were detected covertly buying dollars for yen to stem the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's potentially export-damaging rise against the weakening greenback. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said Tuesday's pullback was only temporary and the dollar was likely to resume its downward spiral, possibly bringing the euro back to its 1999 launch level of $1.1747 soon. \par \par \uc2  "There is no fresh news today, no real data," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank in London. "And we saw euro/dollar drift lower on some profit-taking," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit session highs at $1.1604 per euro, drifting around $1.1610-30 throughout the European session - slightly stronger from New York's closing levels and more than a cent above Monday's four-year lows of $1.1738. \par \par \uc2  It was also slightly up versus the yen at 117.24, after suspected Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took it up from two year lows around 115.10 yen in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  "The main factor is whether or not there will be further signals of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar/yen and whether or not (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is satisfied at the current level," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  But technical analysts said charts pointed to fresh dollar-selling just around the corner. \par \par \uc2  "The short-term (euro/dollar) uptrend remains intact and $1.1740 can be revisited," Barclays Capital Research analyst Phil Roberts wrote in a client note. \par \par \uc2  "Only if euro/dollar sinks back below $1.1595 would the implication be that we are in for a repeat of the type of corrective rally which we saw last week." \par \par \uc2  POLICY VIEWS \par \par \uc2  Investors are closely watching fresh policy signals from Japan and the United States after Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  action should be taken if there were unnatural moves in the market. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are concerned gains in the yen will stymie export growth, the only bright spot in the stagnating economy, and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  detected on Monday seemed the most aggressive action this year. \par \par \uc2  "I think this proved their strong commitment to protect 115 yen," said Katsunori Kitakura, treasury manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have declined to comment on whether they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday but they did so earlier this year to curb yen gains. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that each time the dollar has fallen towards 115 yen in the past two years, the authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A congressional testimony by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow at 1400 GMT will also be closely watched later on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The latest dollar plunge came after Snow and fellow Group of Seven policymakers appeared unconcerned at the weekend by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rapid decline. Snow also said that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves had been "fairly modest". \par \par \uc2  The market is also awaiting Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's views on the shape of the economy when he testifies before the congressional Joint Economic Committee on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In the euro zone, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member and Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke raised expectations the ECB may be positioning for lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  He told a newspaper he saw no sign of deflation in Germany or Europe and there was room for manoeuvre in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030520dz5k00k82\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
