\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar supported by suspected BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2003\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar was holding above 117 yen in early Asian trading on Tuesday after suspected heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped lift it off two-year lows but dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide could resume soon. \par \par \uc2  The euro backed off after rallying to near its January 1999 launch level of $1.1747, reaching a high of $1.1738 in post-Asian trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "There is plenty of interest by exporters to sell the dollar after it rose above 117 yen for the first time in a week, presumably due to Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a trader at a major Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said suspected yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the dollar rebound to as high as 117.50 yen after it dropped to a two-year low near 115 yen on Monday. \par \par \uc2  At 2330 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 117.11/16 yen  compared with Monday's late U.S. level of 117.23. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last detected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 116.50 yen and slightly below 117. \par \par \uc2  "For now, the market is careful about selling the dollar too heavily below 116.50 yen in the wake of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the trader said. "But the dollar is likely to drift towards 115 yen given its weak undertone." \par \par \uc2  The dollar received some help from comments by St. Louis Federal Reserve President William Poole who said the U.S. economy had the potential to grow much more quickly and that the risk of deflation in the United States was low. \par \par \uc2  Poole's remark contrasted with a comment by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke's to Die Welt newspaper. Welteke said that if recent developments in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates continued, that may open up more room for monetary policy actions. \par \par \uc2  Welteke's comments helped take some heat away from the red-hot euro, intensifying speculation the ECB will cut interest rates at its next policy-setting meeting in early June. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1636/41  at 2330 GMT against a late New York level of $1.1645. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed off from a record high of 136.85 yen recorded in post-Asian time, but the underlying trend was bullish after it decisively broke above the key level of 135.50. The euro stood at 136.28/31 yen against 136.43/55. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030519dz5j0197u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
