\par\par \b \uc2  CHRONOLOGY-History of  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2003\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 19 (Reuters) - The following is a chronology of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by major central banks on the dollar, yen, mark and euro. \par \par \uc2  May 19, 2003 - Dealers in Tokyo say Japanese monetary authorities bought dollars for yen at around 115.10. In European midsession, dollar climbs more than a full yen from 115.25 in a short space of time. Japan declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  May 15, 2003 - Dealers in London and New York say Japanese monetary authorities sold yen for dollars when the yen rose to a 10-month high of 115.33 in early New York trade. Japanese monetary authorities declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  May 13, 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  detected conducting yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the dollar fell as low as 116.36 yen in late Tokyo trade. There was no confirmation from the Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  January-March, 2003 - Data on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed Japanese authorities spent 2.5 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from January to March. \par \par \uc2  February 28, 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry confirms it has conducted solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second straight month, buying dollars and euros worth about 513 billion yen. It said it had asked the Bank of Japan to step into the market several times in late February and that euro-buying had been "far less" than dollar-buying versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  Mid-January, 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under newly-appointed top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi, spending a little less than 700 billion yen days after the yen rose to a four-month high around 117.40 against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed the action later in January. \par \par \uc2  June 28, 2002 - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, and U.S. Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sell yen on BOJ's behalf early in New York trading. ECB confirms it buys euros for yen. Traders see sharp euro move from around 118.10 to highs above 119. Dealers also see Fed buying dollars for yen from around 119, lifting the dollar to highs around 120.35. BOJ data released in July showed Japan spent about 520.5 billion yen to weaken the yen on this day alone. \par \par \uc2  June 26, 2002 - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, lifting the dollar to 121.35 from around 120.20-30. Second bout takes the dollar to around 121.10 from 120.50/60. Third bout in early European session takes dollar to 120.70 from 120.03. BOJ is estimated to have bought $18.56 billion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, including May 31. \par \par \uc2  June 24, 2002 - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in early afternoon Tokyo trading, lifting the dollar to around 122.80 from 121.10/15 as the greenback fell to a seven-month low below 121 in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  June 4, 2002 - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, lifting the dollar a full yen from 123.35 yen. \par \par \uc2  May 31, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars as the dollar falls to around 123 yen, after it dropped to a six-month low below 123 in the previous session. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes it back up to 124.45/50 before another slide to 124 and another round of BOJ yen-selling. Third bout takes it from around 123.75 to above 124. \par \par \uc2  May 23, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for second straight day, seen selling yen for dollars at 123.90/95. \par \par \uc2  May 22, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars, after the dollar fell to a 5-1/2 month low of 123.50 on doubts about the speed of the U.S. economic recovery. BOJ seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 123.80/90 yen per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Sept 24/26/27/28, 2001 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars, worrying about an export crippling rise in the value of the yen and following attacks on U.S. cities on September 11. Sept 24/26/27 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not limited to the dollar but includes buying euros for yen, with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and euro zone central banks operating on behalf of BOJ. On Sept 27 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the New York Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf for the first time in the September round. \par \par \uc2  Sept 17, 19, 21, 2001 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Nov 10, 2000 - ECB in conjunction with national euro zone central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros. \par \par \uc2  Nov 6, 2000 - ECB and other euro zone national central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros. \par \par \uc2  Nov 3, 2000 - ECB and other euro zone national central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least twice to buy euros, following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five percent recovery from record lows set the previous week around $0.8225. \par \par \uc2  Sept 22, 2000 - Central banks in Europe, Japan and the United States, acting together for first time since 1995, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive euro higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits all-time low below 85 cents two days earlier, a loss of nearly 30 percent of its value since its January 1999 launch. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is first by ECB since its birth. \par \par \uc2  January 1999 to April 2000 - Japan concerned too-strong yen, trading around 108 to the dollar in January, 1999, will choke off fragile economic recovery and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to tame its strength. Bank of Japan (BOJ) sells yen at least 18 times in this time period, including once via the Federal Reserve and once via the ECB. Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , yen continues to strengthen against dollar, reaching 102 by April 2000. \par \par \uc2  April-June 1998 - With yen weakening, BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As yen crumples below 144 to the dollar, U.S. authorities on June 17, 1998 join the BOJ, spending $833 million buying yen. \par \par \uc2  April 1994 - August 1995 - Dollar sinks to record lows against the German mark, reaching 1.41 in July 1995, and it hits post-World-War Two lows against the yen below 83 to the dollar in April 1995. From April 1994, United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly, often in concert with Japanese and individual European central banks, to prop up U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The last coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with European banks in this period takes place on August 15, 1995. \par \par \uc2  April-August 1993 - U.S. buys dollars and sells yen. \par \par \uc2  1991-1992 - U.S. and European central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly as U.S. economy tumbles into recession during Gulf War, which weakens the dollar. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both sides of market, spending more than $2.5 billion buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and selling $750 million. \par \par \uc2  1988-1990 - Dollar trends higher. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Group of Seven statements on importance of maintaining exchange rate stability. \par \par \uc2  February 1987 - Louvre Accord. Weak dollar, growing U.S. trade deficit and prospect of weakening U.S. economy raises concerns in Europe and Japan about further dollar weakening. G5 plus Italy meet at the Louvre in Paris and issue statement agreeing to "foster stability of exchange rates around current levels." United States, frequently in coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  a number of times to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  September 1985 - Plaza Accord. Group of Five - U.S., Germany, Japan, Britain, France - meet at Plaza Hotel in New York to discuss concerns about very strong dollar and U.S. worries about declining competitiveness. Following weeks see substantial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales of dollars for other G5 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by U.S. and other monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  1980-1981 - U.S. authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame strengthened dollar. \par \par \uc2  1978-1979 - Dollar comes under heavy pressure amid high oil prices, high U.S. inflation and deteriorating balance of payments. In November 1978 a major new dollar support programme is introduced to raise a large war chest of up to $30 billion. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcefully and often in the market with other central banks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030519dz5j00o8d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
