\par\par \b \uc2  CHRONOLOGY-Milestones for the euro since its launch. \b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2003\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 19 (Reuters) - The euro hit a new four-year high above $1.17 on Monday, coming within a whisker of its $1.1747 January 1999 launch level. \par \par \uc2  Following is a chronology showing the fall and rise of Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over its short but active lifetime. \par \par \uc2  * January 1999 - Euro begins trading at $1.1747 on January 4 and rises on the same day to around $1.19, a level that remains its all-time high. It then begins a decline fuelled both by dollar strength and a perceived reluctance by the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) to respond to political and market pressure for lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  * March 1999 - German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine resigns. A few days later a financial mismanagement scandal prompts the resignation of the European Commission en masse. The euro slides three cents during the month to end at around $1.08. \par \par \uc2  * April 1999 - Euro falls below $1.08 as the ECB makes the first monetary policy move of its career, lowering interest rates by half a percentage point to 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  * December 1999 - Euro falls to one-for-one with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  * September 2000 - Euro falls below $0.85. Central banks in Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing its weakness may destabilise the world economy. \par \par \uc2  * October 2000 - Effect of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proves short-lived as euro slides to a record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value. \par \par \uc2  * November 2000 - Euro rises to $0.86 as national central banks in the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros alongside the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  * January 2001 - Euro rises above $0.95 as concerns mount over the state of the U.S. economy and the U.S. Federal Reserve cuts interest rates by half a point to 6.0 percent, the start of an aggressive rate-cutting campaign. The euro trades in a range between $0.84 and $0.95 for the next seven months. \par \par \uc2  * September 2001 - Euro rises two percent to $0.92 on September 11 as two hijacked planes hit the twin towers of New York's World Trade Centre. The euro eases towards the end of that year, ending 2001 around $0.88. \par \par \uc2  * January, 2002 - The euro leaps above $0.90 on the first trading day of the year, buoyed by a wave of enthusiasm inspired by the smooth introduction of euro notes and coins in the 12 countries of the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  * June 2002 - Euro extends gains above $0.96 as the United States shows a record current-account deficit in the first quarter and U.S. telecommunications giant WorldCom and office equipment maker Xerox follow energy giant Enron in revealing accounting irregularities. \par \par \uc2  * July 2002 - Euro regains the one-to-one level against the dollar as concern over the outlook for Wall Street encourages investors to move out of U.S. assets. Euro zone finance ministers issue a formal statement saying they welcome the euro's rise. \par \par \uc2  * December 2002 - Euro ends the month near $1.05, up almost 18 percent on the year on mounting concerns that geopolitical uncertainty will make it hard for the United States to attract enough foreign investment to fund its rising trade gap. \par \par \uc2  * March 2003 - Euro rises above $1.10 on jitters ahead of an apparently imminent U.S.-led war against Iraq but slips back to $1.06 in the first week of April as a speedy U.S. victory looks likely. \par \par \uc2  * April/early May 2003 - Euro gains 10 cents against the dollar in the space of five weeks, rising above its launch levels against the yen and British pound. Dollar struggles across the board as worries over the U.S. current account and budget deficits resurface after end of the Iraq war. \par \par \uc2  May 8 - ECB President Wim Duisenberg says there is nothing excessive about the euro's current level. \par \par \uc2  May 11 - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow notes a cheap dollar would help U.S. exports, helping lift the euro above $1.16 for first time in over four years. \par \par \uc2  May 17 - Speaking after a Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting, Snow says the dollar's slide represents a "really fairly modest" realignment in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values, giving traders the green light to push the euro above $1.17. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030519dz5j00idn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
