\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Rebounds Amid Talk of BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2003\par 00:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- After plunging to multiyear lows on comments by the U.S. Treasury Secretary, the dollar boomeranged back into the black against the yen and recovered the bulk of its losses against the euro Monday, spurred by a suspected Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was at 117.34 yen, up more than 2 yen from its global session low of 115.12 yen, and up steeply from 115.96 yen late Friday. The euro was at $1.1658, off the fresh four-year high of $1.1738 it touched intraday trade, but up from $1.1577 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.2968 Swiss francs, off its global session low of $1.2903 -- its lowest level since October 1998 -- and down from 1.3069 Swiss francs late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6325, compared with $1.6227 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary John Snow's remarks to reporters over the weekend at the Group of Eight finance ministers' meeting in France unleashed a wave of dollar selling early in the global session, as market participants digested the implications of a nuanced change in the U.S. dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Not only did Mr. Snow appear nonplussed by recent declines by the dollar, he also told reporters that the government's definition of a strong dollar doesn't refer to its value against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This marked a shift in the U.S.'s handling of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, opening the door for further weakening. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the Bush administration insisted there has been no change in its policy on the dollar and it still supports a strong dollar. "The dollar policy is unchanged. There is no change involved in the dollar. The government supports a strong dollar," White House spokesman Ari Fleischer said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the official lip service to the "strong dollar" policy, market participants have focused instead on the apparent lack of U.S. concern over the dollar's decline, taking Mr. Snow's comments as a signal to continue selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide, however, was thought to have been cut short Monday by Japanese authorities who prefer their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be the underdog of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  world. After bouncing around global session lows of $115.12, the dollar suddenly rocketed higher when New York traders began arriving Monday morning, pushing through 116 yen within an hour and well past 117 yen during the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been launching so-called stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which it places orders to buy dollars with banks rather than executing them on its own or through another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , starting last week, market participants believe. Monday's steep reversal in the dollar indicated that the BOJ stepped up its defense of a weak yen, coming into the market with a "good size" of dollar-buy orders, said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists believe the sharp dollar gains versus the yen spilled into the greenback's standing against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Profit-taking on gains by the euro and Swiss franc also kept the dollar from revisiting its global session lows. \par \par \uc2  In light of Mr. Snow's comments and the apparent comfort most industrialized nations' have with current dollar weakness, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists, like Mr. Chandler of HSBC, believe a "mini-Plaza agreement" is in place, referring to the Plaza Accord reached in 1985, under which industrialized nations would coordinate their efforts to weaken the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Other strategists contend, however, that nothing could be further from the truth: The U.S. is acting alone, they say, and the Snow comments represent not only a shift in dollar policy, but a shift in the political winds at home. "The U.S. is definitely driving the bus in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," said Marcel Kasumovich, economist at Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  "If they were able to reach a consensus there would have been some kind of action," he said, adding that the status quo -- which is a trend toward a weaker dollar -- indicates that there is no agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  Bob Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York, shares that view and believes the Bush administration is being motivated by two factors: \par \par \uc2  - The U.S. wants the rest of the world to shoulder more responsibility when it comes to global growth. A weaker dollar will force other nations to focus efforts on domestic growth, since they will be unable to export their way to higher output. \par \par \uc2  - The administration also "doesn't want to get into a position where there is some level of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate that they want to defend," which could complicate efforts to get the economy up and running when deflation looms on the horizon, said Mr. Sinche. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts believe Mr. Snow's remarks in and of themselves represent a red herring. What's more significant for some observers, is that Mr. Snow has been given the latitude to make these comments, without being reprimanded, as had been the case when his predecessor veered from the "strong dollar" script. \par \par \uc2  "None of this is deliberate," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Conn, noting that Snow's comments were made to reporters, not in prepared speeches. \par \par \uc2  "I would say that the emergence of some tolerance [by the White House] for Snow freeing up the language on dollar policy reflects a political agenda which is: by all means necessary, grow the economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  While European authorities have indicated that they are comfortable with current levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists have warned that a climb of the euro above $1.20 in the near term would likely ignite "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," where officials begin discouraging further strengthening. \par \par \uc2  But, for the time being, officials such as European Union Commissioner Pedro Solbes maintain that a strong, stable euro remains in the interests of the euro zone and the western European economy. \par \par \uc2  Write to Agnes T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-19-03 1848ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030519dz5j007ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
