\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL YIELD: Aggressive BoJ Fuels Rally In Treasurys \b0\par\par\uc2  By Joy C. Shaw \par \uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2003\par 21:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- A sharp spike in the dollar against Japanese yen Monday morning was accompanied by yet another rally in Treasurys, prompting market participants to speculate about large-scale buying by the Bank of Japan in the U.S. bond market. \par \par \uc2  Between 7 a.m. (1100 GMT) and 8 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), the dollar surged by over one full yen to Y116.70 from Y115.22. At the same time, Treasurys rallied from already historically low yield levels, setting a new record low in the 30-year bond yield at 4.38%. The yield on the benchmark 10-year note also sunk to a new 45-year low at 3.37%. \par \par \uc2  Even though the Treasury market quickly lost its gains as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stabilized later in the morning, the day's movement highlights the role that Japan's monetary policy is playing as one of the main sources driving U.S. interest rates lower. \par \par \uc2  Monday's covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is part of the  Japanese government's effort to weaken the yen whenever the dollar moves to the Y115.15 area, according to market participants. \par \par \uc2  What's more, the current wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be more aggressive than previous ones this year and in 2002, participants say. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimate the amount the BoJ spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between May 8 and May 16 alone at around $20 billion, almost matching the amount spent in the entire first quarter. \par \par \uc2  And while the actual amount spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be confirmed until the end of May, many in the market say the BoJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday may have been one of its largest one-day purchases of dollar assets, to the tune of $5 billion or more, given the magnitude of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move. \par \par \uc2  "This may be the heaviest day of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a long time, maybe the biggest," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEA Global in New York. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the dollar continued to gain against the yen throughout New York trading, hitting a high of Y117.29 by midafternoon Monday. Traders attributed that to more covert buying by the BoJ around Y116.55 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Aggressive BoJ Keeps Global Yields Low \par \par \uc2  For the global bond market, an aggressive BoJ, mindful of the competitiveness of Japanese corporations and deflation in its economy, means that global interest rates, especially those in the U.S., won't likely rise as long as dollar is in a weakening trend. \par \par \uc2  Analysts at Montreal-based BCA Research said the current rally in U.S. Treasurys indicates "the extreme imbalance in the global economy and the resulting policy conflicts." \par \par \uc2  "In the U.S., the weak dollar has become the chief conduit for monetary reflation," the analysts said in a report. "This is a problem for (Japanese) officials who have made it clear that they don't want a stronger yen." \par \par \uc2  As a result, massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yen from rallying has been the policy response from Japan, they said. \par \par \uc2  In order to effectively change the dynamics driving the dollar-yen pair, the BoJ's purchases during \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been mostly in Treasurys and agency paper. \par \par \uc2  IDEA's Callow estimates about 96% of the amount the BoJ spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the first quarter went into U.S. government securities and only 4% was used to buy euro-denominated sovereign bonds. \par \par \uc2  "It is not surprising that last week's break in the dollar triggered another surge in bond prices because it forced the Japanese authorities to buy even more Treasurys to prevent the yen from following the euro higher," said BCA analysts. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, the BoJ isn't the only reason why Treasurys have shot through the roof recently. \par \par \uc2  Sung Won Sohn, chief economic officer at Wells Fargo Bank in Minneapolis, said the effect of deflation talk, triggered by the statement published after the Federal Open Market Committee's meeting on May 6, had a more significant impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  "Bond investors believe it's a win-win situation that the Fed will cut interest rates and even buy bonds," he said. \par \par \uc2  Since deflation concerns won't disappear right away, yields on Treasurys will stay low in the foreseeable future, Sohn said, adding that over time as the BoJ continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bond yields will fall even further. \par \par \uc2  But there is a downside risk involved too, as the BoJ's appetite for Treasurys could disappear as quickly as it emerged. \par \par \uc2  "The unsustainability of this policy should be evident," BCA analysts said. "Both authorities are working to boost growth. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works to depress global bond yields which will reverse as soon as (the) policy appears to be working." \par \par \uc2  (Joy Shaw covers Treasurys and global debt markets for Dow Jones Newswires) \par \par \uc2  -By Joy C. Shaw; 201-938-2137; joy.shaw@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030519dz5j009kh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
