\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Markets Expected To Weaken After Resona Bailout \b0\par\par\uc2  By Iain McDonald \par \uc2 1,245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2003\par 12:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's stock and credit markets will likely start weaker in nervous trading Monday as investors move to the sidelines to assess the impact of the government's bailout of the country's fifth largest bank, Resona Holdings Inc. (J.RSN or 8308), announced over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The yen could also suffer on foreign investor concerns about Japan's banking system as well as an apparent green light from a weekend Group of Eight meeting of finance ministers for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But any market sell-off will be moderated by the nature of the government's planned fund injection and bailout program, which protects all Resona creditors, including shareholders. The move confirms analysts' assumptions that the government is determined to stop any major Japanese bank from going under. \par \par \uc2  The government said Saturday it will inject an as yet undetermined amount of cash into Resona Bank to push up the bank's capital adequacy ratio above 10% from its current level of just over 2%, which is below the 4% required for banks to operate in Japan's domestic markets. Local media have said around Y2 trillion in taxpayer money will ultimately be poured into the bank. \par \par \uc2  Resona, already weakened by falling share prices and bad-loan write-offs, was hit by tougher accounting standards as part of the government's plan to clean up the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  Despite the bailout, Resona, which was formed through the merger of Daiwa Bank, Asahi Bank and other smaller banks, will continue to operate as a bank, though with a change of management, while the government will fully protect depositors. \par \par \uc2  "This is a sobering reminder that all isn't well in Japan and we shouldn't be complacent," said Jason Rogers, credit analyst at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Shares May Weaken Initially\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stock prices will likely fall Monday because the bailout shows the\par frailty of Japan's banking system but doesn't yet suggest the government\par will do much that is different to reform it, says Hiromichi Shirakawa,\par chief economist at UBS Warburg.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't think this measure will accelerate reform in the banking\par sector or corporate sector but is merely to maintain economic\par stability," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, others say share market participants could be more relieved\par than worried by the government's quick move to help Resona stay in\par business.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market will get the impression that Resona has received a seal of\par approval from the government," to get back to a sound financial\par standing, said stock market commentator Yasuo Ueki. So Resona shares\par could draw buying interest.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If Japan were near its fiscal half- or full-year book closing at the\par end of March and September and the stock market were fragile, then share\par prices may have fallen sharply, he said. But "I don't think the Nikkei\par will fall below the 8000 threshold," given the recent strong performance\par on Wall Street, he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei 225 Stock Average shed 6.11 points, or 0.1%, Friday to\par close at 8117.29, well above a 20-year low set last month of 7603.76.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei might start lower, falling at most 100 to 150 points, but\par could recoup those losses later in the session, Ueki added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Corporate Bond Spreads May Widen Initially\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the bond market, spreads between yields on Japanese government\par bonds and riskier corporate bonds will likely widen due to uncertainty.\par But the weakness may not last.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Even though there mightn't be much trading, spreads could be pushed\par out until the dust settles," said Barclays' Rogers. Investors will now\par wonder whether any other of Japan's banks are in a similar trouble\par although the government said that the other lenders are in fine shape.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But stepping back, the bigger picture is that the government was\par immediately there with funds," confirming that Japan's financial\par institutions have strong government backing, underpinning their credit\par ratings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Daiwa and Asahi Banks haven't been issuers of straight bonds for some\par time but do have a range of outstanding perpetual bonds and subordinated\par debt, Rogers said. Investors holding those securities won't be affected\par by the bailout.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Japanese government bond market, the effects of Resona's\par difficulties could be both a positive and a negative factor.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bailout may further drive investors to the safe haven of JGBs but\par it also shows that the market's biggest participants - banks - may in\par the long-term scale back their involvement as they restructure.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also, increasing costs for the government in supporting the banks\par threatens to boost JGB supply. That could prompt a change in course for\par JGB prices, which have already risen to record high levels in recent\par sessions, with the yield on Japan's benchmark 10-year JGB ending 1.5\par basis points lower Friday at 0.575%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Yen Likely To Buck Trend Of General Dlr Weakness\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Japan has said it will provide whatever liquidity is\par necessary to keep money markets and the overall financial system stable,\par but money market participants will still be watching for any funding\par bottlenecks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After an emergency policy board meeting Saturday, the BOJ said it will\par provide Resona with immediate funds if needed although the bank hasn't\par yet had any liquidity problems.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy board is due to hold its regular policy\par board meeting Monday and Tuesday and will watch financial markets\par closely to gauge whether any monetary easing is necessary. Ahead of the\par weekend, analysts had expected the BOJ would keep policy settings stable\par but possibly issue details of its planned asset-backed securities\par purchase program.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, renewed uncertainty about the strength of\par Japan's financial institutions could push the yen lower against the\par dollar. That would be against a more general trend of expected dollar\par weakness following the failure of G8 ministers to publicly express\par concern about recent dollar weakness against the euro or yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "A country with a banking system in crisis rarely has an appreciating\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Marshall Gittler, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.\par "Banking crises and appreciating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  don't come together."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Apparent strict application of accounting rules to Resona's balance\par sheet could spur speculation that a closer look at other Japanese\par companies will reveal similar problems, he added. "That's quite negative\par for the yen."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G8 finance ministers' meeting in Deauville, aside from appearing\par to ignore dollar weakness, made no mention of recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap the dollar's losses against the yen, implying the\par world's major powers are for now willing to accept Japan's direct action\par to stop the strengthening of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, so far, is predicting there\par won't be any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market impact from the events surrounding\par Resona.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There won't be any reverberations on other financial institutions,"\par he told reporters in France after the G8 meeting. "At this point, I\par absolutely don't sense any impact on the economy, therefore, I don't see\par any impact on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In late U.S. trading Friday, the dollar was quoted at Y115.96, near\par its low for the day at Y115.80.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Hiroyuki Kachi and Arran Scott in Tokyo and James Simms in Deauville,\par France contributed to this story.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030518dz5i0006g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
