\par\par \b \uc2  G7 shows scant public concern for falling dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Dolan \par \uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2003\par 22:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DEAUVILLE, France, May 17 (Reuters) - The world's finance chiefs on Saturday omitted referring to volatile world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a post-meeting public statement, a move that may be seen by markets as endorsing the recent U.S. dollar slide. \par \par \uc2  Ministers from the Group of Seven (G7) richest nations, famous for its dramatic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1980s, told reporters after the meeting they agreed to avoid explicit reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets due to the absence of their central bankers. \par \par \uc2  Meetings without the bankers avoid written references to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. \par \par \uc2  They also said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were discussed as part of a general economic assessment and there was agreement to continue to monitor markets and cooperate if action was necessary - a line that is often included in their communiques. \par \par \uc2  But the absence of this standard reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is marked at a time when the dollar has fallen steeply to near four-year lows against the euro and two-year lows against Japan's yen and is likely to fuel the view the G7 is relaxed about these trends. \par \par \uc2  Last year's pre-summit meeting of G7 finance ministers in Halifax, Canada also omitted any reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when central bankers where absent, but the dollar drop has accelerated sharply since then and has caused concerns in Japan and Europe. Comments on Saturday from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow stoked the view that governments are comfortable with current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates - showing that the United States at least was not strongly opposed to the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  "There was a general discussion of the backdrop against which the world economy is functioning, and part of that backdrop is the really fairly modest realignment of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has occurred," Snow told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Snow, who added pressure the dollar's decline this week by saying a weaker dollar was good for U.S. exports, has frequently qualified the U.S. Treasury's long-standing "strong dollar" policy from Clinton administration in the 1990's boom years. \par \par \uc2  Snow's description of a "strong" dollar on Saturday also revealed that a high exchange rate with the world's other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not necessarily part of his view. \par \par \uc2  Asked what he meant by "strong" in Washington's long-running policy, Snow said: "What you want to be strong is (that) you want people to have confidence in your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you want them to see a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a good medium of exchange." \par \par \uc2  "You want the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a good store of value. You want it to be something people are wiling to hold. You want it to be hard to counterfeit...those are the qualities," he added. \par \par \uc2  Pressed to say whether he considered the value of the dollar to be among the qualities that he used to define a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Snow said, "That reflects the fundamentals of the demand and supply for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  EUROPEAN, JAPANESE SILENCE \par \par \uc2  But with suspicion widespread that the U.S. is happy to see a weaker dollar aid its exports and economic recovery, and ultimately its yawing balance of payments deficit, the relative silence from Japan and Europe was as noteworthy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is down almost 10 percent this year against the euro and this is already drawing complaints from European exporters who are also dealing with stagnant economies. \par \par \uc2  The meeting's host, French Finance Minister Francis Mer, and British counterpart Gordon Brown both indicated exchange rates were discussed merely to the point that monitoring of markets was ongoing and cooperation was agreed if necessary. \par \par \uc2  But none of the ministers revealed any particular discomfort and euro zone officials stuck steadfastly to their official line from a meeting earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone finance ministers, with the backing of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Monday a strong and stable euro was in the interests of the regional and global economies. \par \par \uc2  "A strong and stable euro is in the interests of the euro area and the global economy. Naturally the exchange rate of the euro needs to reflect economic fundamentals," Euro Group chairman Nikos Christodoulakis said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Even though the yen is only down about two percent against the dollar so this year, unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter involved purchasing some $20 billion on the open market and billions more were likely bought since then. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, preoccupied with a crisis back home at one of the country's leading banks, made no mention of the yen on Saturday. Following a bilateral with Snow on Friday, he merely said the G7 meeting was unlikely to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The absence of Japanese comment is unlikely to mean it will cease its unilateral battle to cap the yen against the dollar but there was no sign of any international support. \par \par \uc2  Coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often seen as most effective when conducted with an element of surprise, but any concerted action now would be extraordinary given the weekend comments. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030517dz5h00gem\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
