\par\par \b \uc2  Firming yen puts Japan exporters, leaders in pinch. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2003\par 00:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 15 (Reuters) - The dollar's slide and the yen's spike has wedged Japan's exporters and policy makers between a rock and a hard place. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar's slip below key levels to more than two-year lows against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be poised to steepen its fall, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts expect. \par \par \uc2  As the world's second biggest economy struggles to grow, the firming yen - buoyed by Japan's hefty current account surplus - is threatening to erode the competitive edge of Japanese companies that sell their goods abroad. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government's short-term solution to this economic fix is hefty yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yet even substantial sales of yen against dollars may not be enough to stem the tide of broader greenback weakness for long. \par \par \uc2  Responding to traders' strong suspicions that Japan was selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to weaken it on Thursday, Japan's monetary authorities declined to comment. Yet market analysts said the dollar's abrupt recovery from session lows at 115.33 yen toward 116.0 yen likely reflected the Bank of Japan's efforts to prevent the dollar from slipping below the 115.0 mark - oft cited as a critical level for Japan's lethargic economy. \par \par \uc2  LINE IN THE SAND \par \par \uc2  "That's a serious line in the sand for (Japanese) policy makers: They are going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very, very heavily around those levels," said Leo Kropywiansky, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Putnam Investments in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan sold some $20 billion worth of yen during the first quarter in order to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After a hiatus in April, the BOJ is once again prepared to step in and spend whatever necessary to continue the campaign, Kropywiansky said. The BOJ acts on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although the BOJ is a behemoth in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, its yen-weakening efforts are not driving a broad array of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants to push the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down in a sustained way. \par \par \uc2  "There were pretty widespread rumors the BOJ was active from the sub-115.50 (dollar/yen) level, trying to push the dollar back toward 116.0 yen," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with IDEAglobal in New York. "But then there was a conspicuous lack of follow-through," he said. Both shorter-term investors, including hedge funds, and longer-term investors have shown a tendency to sell dollars against yen recently and that pressure is likely to continue, Delano added. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR TEETERING? \par \par \uc2  Technical strategists believe the yen has pushed the dollar to the edge of a cliff. \par \par \uc2  "Any chart detective is looking at the very crucial 115.50 yen level," said Jordan Kotick, technical strategist with JP Morgan in New York. "You have to be aware of the budding bearish potential (for the dollar against the yen)" he said. At least one weekly close of the dollar below that mark - which the greenback slipped briefly below on Thursday - could bring the dollar down to between 112.0 yen and 110.0 yen, Kotick said. \par \par \uc2  For the moment, the dollar is still sitting on the edge. \par \par \uc2  The floor of the greenback's new lower trading range is around 115.20 yen, said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  After Japanese finance minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Thursday his country's government needed to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent speculative moves, that standard reminder of the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  temporarily lifted the dollar higher, to around 116.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Speaking ahead of the Group of Seven (G7) leading economies' finance ministers meeting in France on Friday and Saturday, Shiokawa's remarks also serve as a reminder that the United States and Japan may be quietly at loggerheads over Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market actions. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow recently indicated he has no sympathy for Japan's efforts to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts now regard the U.S. administration's commitment to a strong dollar as defunct in all but name. \par \par \uc2  Traders' prevailing view that the U.S. administration will tolerate a further decline of the dollar may ultimately drag the greenback below the next, critical support area against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people will turn more negative on the dollar if the 115.0 yen level failed to hold," said Chandler. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030516dz5f00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
