\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar supported by BOJ threats, euro drifts down. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2003\par 05:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 15 (Reuters) - The dollar was stuck in tight ranges on Thursday, keeping some distance from its latest 10-month bottom versus the yen as threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset renewed worries about the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro drifted down on speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) might cut rates, with hedge-related selling against the yen from Japanese investors undermining sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stayed under pressure on the yen after falling to around 115.55 yen - its lowest since July - on Wednesday following poor U.S. retail sales data for April. \par \par \uc2  But wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities repelled the speculative selling force. \par \par \uc2  "As fears over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are extremely strong during Tokyo time, there is no speculative dollar-selling, except for actual selling from exporters," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, markets division manager at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very nervous about selling the dollar around the present level below 116.50 yen after seeing it being very solid during Tokyo trade yesterday (on Wednesday)." \par \par \uc2  As of 0230 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 116.18/23 yen slightly weaker than the 116.30 seen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Despite the support above 116 yen, the greenback was fragile as the market has seen it lose ground in post-Asian trade for the past several days. \par \par \uc2  The dollar came under strong pressure on Wednesday after U.S. retail sales fell 0.1 percent in April compared with a forecast for a 0.4 percent increase. Sales excluding autos fell 0.9 percent, the biggest drop since September 2001. \par \par \uc2  The data indicated that retail sales, a pillar of the U.S. economy, might be showing some fatigue despite the end of hostilities in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "It (the dollar) could slip again in post-Asian trade as some players think Japan has changed its stance to smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than blocking the yen's rise," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president at UFJ Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may scale back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spread this week after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow made critical remarks about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an interview with Reuters. But some operators said the Japanese authorities remain determined to stop any yen rise above 115 per dollar, a level seen as the break-even point for many Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they have changed their stance at all. I think they will act when they want to," said Sahara. \par \par \uc2  EURO DIPS \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors showed appetite for foreign bonds. Government data showed Japanese investors bought 1.5374 trillion yen ($13.22 billion) of foreign bonds last week, the largest net buying ever. \par \par \uc2  But such buying could be slowing as the euro has lost momentum since it hit a four-year high of $1.1624 earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Speculation has grown that the ECB might cut rates as the euro's strength has drawn complaints from European firms and government officials. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1448/54 from around $1.1480 in late U.S. trade. Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 133.12/17 yen, compared with 133.52 yen \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, French Finance Minister Francis Mer chimed in, saying the euro's rise against the dollar was "not very favourable in terms of (European) growth" and that there was room for movement on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  ECB Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said on Tuesday the bank may have room for rate cuts, if oil prices and the dollar stayed low. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit a record high of 135.49 yen on Monday, but speculation over an ECB credit easing have induced hedge selling from Japanese investors. \par \par \uc2  "As credit-easing speculation increased after Welteke's comments and reflecting recent falls in the euro, Japanese institutions - which started buying euros from the start of the fiscal year (in April) - are aggressively hedging their euro positions," said a senior trader at a Japanese bank. ($1=116.25 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030515dz5f005ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
