\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Closes Higher, Edging Back From Multi-Year Lows \b0\par\par\uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2003\par 00:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  The dollar shot higher in thin afternoon trading, when investors decided to book profits on their long euro positions amid growing talk of an interest-rate cut in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the greenback rose from a two-year low against the yen following what many believe to have been covert \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A mixed bag of U.S. economic data, which included a 1.9% decrease in wholesale inflation in April and a moderate decline in initial jobless claims, also proved mildly dollar supportive, according to strategists. \par \par \uc2  In late dealings Thursday, the dollar was at 116.41 yen, up steeply from its session low of 115.35 yen, its lowest point since February 2001. The gain was more moderate when compared with its late-Wednesday level of 116.19 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1391, well off its intraday high of $1.1492 and down sharply from $1.1503 late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.6209 compared with $1.6175 late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  As the U.S. dollar's afternoon rally branched out, the Canadian dollar fell sharply. Late Thursday, the U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3840, up from late Wednesday's C$1.3738, its lowest level since Oct. 8, 1997. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, comments from German officials suggested to some that political pressure is growing on the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower rates to help counter the sharp appreciation of the euro. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stifle already lackluster growth in the euro zone since exports become more expensive in foreign markets. \par \par \uc2  After Germany reported a 0.2% first-quarter decline in economic output early in the global session, Finance Minister Hans Eichel urged the ECB to lower short-term rates from its current target of 2.5% as a way to boost economic growth. Later in the day, German Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, who is also a member of the ECB's governing council, said a weak dollar represents a disadvantage for European exporters, according to German news agency VWD. \par \par \uc2  Short-term interest rates in the U.S. are at 1.25%, and the relatively paltry return has weighed heavily on the dollar recently. Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York, said the difference between interest rates in the U.S. and abroad will continue to trump economic reports in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as investors focus on better yields. \par \par \uc2  "The data will matter because it will matter to central banks, but the markets will wait to see how policy makers interpret the data" before acting upon it, she added. \par \par \uc2  Some financial advisers are even telling their customers to ignore economic data altogether when considering the direction of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We've been saying to clients that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not reflect economic fundamentals and that you're just going to get confused by thinking about economic fundamentals," said Laurie Cameron, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with J.P. Morgan Private Bank in New York. She advises investors to diversify into foreign cash and fixed-income securities of those nations where sovereign and high-grade corporate bonds offer better returns. \par \par \uc2  She noted that prior to this year, the market paid close attention to the so-called growth story since most investors had been geared toward equities and conditions that would promote higher stock prices. But with economies still in the doldrums, investors have shifted their take on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve policy makers recently flagged falling prices as a threat to U.S. growth, broadcasting to financial markets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold the federal funds target at 1.25% for the foreseeable future with the possibility of further cuts down the road. \par \par \uc2  Two days later, the ECB also kept its rates unchanged, but comments from its president didn't hint at easing in June. As a result, the euro rallied despite sagging European economies. \par \par \uc2  While some data releases, like Thursday's gloomy German economic report, can still jolt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. producer price index and jobless claims data failed to elicit much of a response. The dollar actually strengthened after the U.S. data releases, even though the PPI declined sharply by 1.9% in April and jobless claims, while improving, are still are high levels. \par \par \uc2  Write to Agnes T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-15-03 1820ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030516dz5f0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
