\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \b0\par\par\uc2 1,231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2003\par 13:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: U.S. appear set to open mixed as investors await a series of U.S. economic reports. The Dow Jones Industrial Average future is up 5 points, and the Nasdaq 100 future is up 2.5 points. Among companies expected to release quarterly results are Dell Computer Corp. (DELL) and Liberty Media Corp. (L). Tom Hougaard of CityIndex is calling for the Dow to open 5 points lower at 8642, and the Nasdaq to open 3 points up at 1537. \par \par \uc2  EUROPEAN MIDDAY MARKETS: European share markets are higher, with London shares up as the market expects a weaker pound to boost growth. German share prices have been helped by surprisingly good results from Deutsche Telekom and ThyssenKrupp. The FTSE 100 index is up 0.2%, while the Frankfurt DAX index is up 0.6%. The dollar is weaker against the yen despite speculation about continued Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . European bonds are higher on news of a small contraction in euro zone first quarter GDP data. \par \par \uc2  ============================ \par \par \uc2  TOP STORIES: \par \par \uc2  NO GROWTH - Euro-zone growth fell to 0.0% in the first quarter compared with the previous quarter, slowing to its worst rate in almost two years. \par \par \uc2  WHAT'S THIS, A TELEKOM PROFIT? - Deutsche Telekom AG (DT) posted its first quarterly profit in almost two years after boosting operating earnings at its wireless and fixed-line divisions and booking gains from asset sales. (News Snap by Taska Manzaroli) \par \par \uc2  GOING DRUG FREE - Akzo Nobel NV (AKZOY) said it plans to get rid of a sizable part of its troubled pharmaceuticals \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggesting it would shift focus to its coatings division instead. (News Snap by Joana Quintanilha and Arent Jan Hesselink) \par \par \uc2  FORGING A PROFIT - German steel and industrial conglomerate ThyssenKrupp AG (G.TKP) posted a 139% jump in second-quarter net profit and surprised investors with an upbeat forecast for the second half. (News Snap by Christiaan Hetzner) \par \par \uc2  VIVA FRENCH BANKING - Societe Generale SA (F.SGF) provided further proof that French banks are battling their way through share market turmoil and a slowing domestic economy, posting a better-than-expected net profit for the first quarter of EUR483 million. (News Snap by Simon Clow) \par \par \uc2  MORE DUTCH PROBLEMS WITH THE U.S. - ING Groep NV (ING) said first-quarter net profit dropped 85% on investment losses, though investors were more worried about a weak performance by the Dutch bank and insurer's key U.S. life \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (News Snap by Neil Moorhouse) \par \par \uc2  BACK TO THE PHONES - Vivendi Universal SA (V) backed up its stated strategy of focusing on phones with strong first-quarter revenue from its telecommunications divisions. Disappointing revenue at Universal Music Group and continued weakness at French broadcaster Canal Plus Groupe, however, continued to weigh on the French media company. (News Snap by Sarah Moore) \par \par \uc2  ============================ \par \par \uc2  STILL TO COME ON THURSDAY: \par \par \uc2  * E.On (EON) reports first-quarter earnings. \par \par \uc2  * Arcelor (L.ARC) reports first-quarter revenue. \par \par \uc2  * EFG Eurobank Ergasias reports first-quarter earnings. \par \par \uc2  * Danubius (R.DNB) reports first-quarter earnings. \par \par \uc2  * U.S. April Producer Price Index is released at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  * U.S. Initial Jobless Claims for May 10 Week are released at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  * U.S. March Business Inventories are released at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  * U.S. April Capacity Utilization is released at 1315 GMT. \par \par \uc2  * U.S. April Industrial Production is released at 1315 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ============================ \par \par \uc2  INSIGHT & ANALYSIS FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES: \par \par \uc2  =THE SKEPTIC: ThyssenKrupp's (G.TKP) upbeat trade forecast could be in for a hammering later this year. (By Brian Truscott) \par \par \uc2  =THE SKEPTIC: Swisscom AG (Z.SCM) became the latest Swiss company to write down its "patriotic" investment in the airline mess called "Swiss" (Z.SWI). (By Dennis Baker) \par \par \uc2  =THE BOTTOM LINE: After months and months of acute pain from tumbling equity markets, Europe's big investment banks at last have something to crow about. They are making a killing from fixed income. (By Simon Clow and Mirela Vlad) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: The dollar faces another pivotal test against the yen as the Bank of Japan continues to defend key technical support at Y116.00. (By Nicholas Hastings) \par \par \uc2  =CHARTING EUROPE: European stock markets broke out to the upside from their inside day patterns Wednesday but I still believe a medium-term top is being formed. (By Axel Rudolph) \par \par \uc2  =FUND MANAGER: Prosperity Capital Management has gone where few fund managers have dared to tread - taking on Russia's bureaucracy and the country's nascent enterprise culture following the collapse of communism. (By Marietta Cauchi) \par \par \uc2  ============================ \par \par \uc2  OTHER NEWS: \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor-designate Mervyn King stressed a gloomier outlook for economic growth in the U.K., citing challenges from weak consumption and manufacturing sectors. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its May monthly bulletin that downside risks to economic growth have diminished with the end of the war in Iraq, although some risks still remain from macroeconomic imbalances outside the euro zone and from the SARS virus. \par \par \uc2  The London Stock Exchange (U.LSE) reported a 5.6% rise in net profit for the fiscal year ending in March, meeting expectations in a tough market. (News Snap by Alen Mattich) \par \par \uc2  Italian Internet provider Tiscali SpA (I.TIS) announced first quarter results generally ahead of expectations but left analysts fearful that the company was running out of organic growth. (News Snap by Federica Bianchi) \par \par \uc2  Airport operator Fraport AG (G.FRP) said first-quarter net profit rose 43% and it still reckons it'll match last year's earnings and passenger numbers in 2003. (News Snap by Susanna Ray) \par \par \uc2  Germany's KarstadtQuelle AG (G.KSQ) said it narrowed its first-quarter loss thanks to one-off gains, although the retailer continues to suffer from a sluggish German economy and its old-fashioned format. (News Snap by Edward Taylor) \par \par \uc2  Swedish fashion retailer Hennes & Mauritz AB (S.HEM) again blamed the weather for sluggish April sales growth, which fell short of market expectations. (News Snap by Maria Akerhielm) \par \par \uc2  Takeover bid target Safeway PLC (U.SFW) reported full-year results in line with forecasts, but said sales growth is likely to slow as the new financial year progresses. (News Snap by James Hall) \par \par \uc2  U.K. private equity company 3i Group PLC (U.TIG) reported negative returns on its investments for the fiscal year after a significant reduction in the value of technology companies backed by the group. (News Snap by Marietta Cauchi) \par \par \uc2  Germany's Linde AG (G.LIN) reported a first-quarter net profit below expectations as the strong exchange rate for the euro and the weak world economic climate put a strain on its core gas and engineering business segment. (News Snap by Klaus Brune) \par \par \uc2  Hungarian oil and natural gas company MOL Rt.'s (R.MOL) earnings more than doubled in the first quarter of this year, but analysts warned not to expect similarly strong results in coming quarters as profit margins winnow and losses at the company's gas business, its Achilles heel, deepen. (News Snap by Margit Feher) \par \par \uc2  Despite a slump in world equity markets that cost it EUR100 million last year, Bank of Ireland PLC (IRE) delivered a 4.5% increase in fiscal 2003 pretax profit on the back of strong performances from its key domestic banking franchise and wholesale financial services unit. (News Snap by Debra Marks) \par \par \uc2  -By Rob Fisher, Dow Jones Newswires, 44-020-7842-9319, rob.fisher@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  70737 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030515dz5f000rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
