\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Ex-MOF Ito: Shiokawa, Snow Might Discuss  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  At G8 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2003\par 10:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in recent days to fight the soaring yen and may seek approval for its efforts from the U.S. authorities at the upcoming Group of Eight meeting, a former Finance Ministry official said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It's natural" for Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa to raise the issue when he meets U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow this weekend, said Takatoshi Ito, a former deputy vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  Ito said Shiokawa will likely sound out Snow to find out where he stands on Tokyo's struggle against the resurgent yen. Snow triggered a collapse in the dollar this week with remarks that traders say seemed to suggest he was wavering on the long-standing strong-dollar policy and with an apparent jab at Tokyo for its strong-arm tactics on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "As political pressure (on Japan), I'm not sure how serious (Snow's remarks were) meant to be. That should be found out," Ito told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not sure that that (Snow's comment) was really a prepared party line of the U.S. government." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said Wednesday he wouldn't bring up the topic of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Snow when the two meet on the sidelines of the G8 meeting in Deauville, France. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments will likely force Shiokawa's hand. The dollar plunged to Y115.56 overnight, its lowest level since last July. At 0830 GMT Thursday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y115.84. \par \par \uc2  Ito said a further rise in the yen may be quite damaging to Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  "That's the reason that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite heavily in recent days," Ito said. \par \par \uc2  With exports one of the few bright spots for Japan's economy, the Finance Ministry has been aggressively selling yen in the market to prevent a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from pulling the rug out from under the economy. \par \par \uc2  Ito said he expects the Finance Ministry to continue its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including its recent unannounced actions, in its ongoing battle to cap the yen's surge. \par \par \uc2  "A weak yen is in the interest of Japan," Ito said, reasoning it supports the nation's exports and helps fight deflation by making imports more expensive in yen terms. \par \par \uc2  Ito said the Bank of Japan should adopt an inflation target of 1% to 3% to be achieved within the next two years to help lift Japan out of nearly four years of deflation. \par \par \uc2  Ito is a well-known advocate of inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has consistently rejected calls for adopting a specific inflation target, saying it doesn't have the necessary tools to achieve such a policy. \par \par \uc2  But Ito said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could print money and purchase assets such as exchange traded funds (ETFs), real estate investment trusts (REITs) and medium-grade corporate bonds and commercial paper to reach inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  Ito, once a dark horse candidate for BOJ governor, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must play a key role in taming deflation by taking such unorthodox monetary easing steps. \par \par \uc2  "(The BOJ) can save Japan. The longer the government and the BOJ wait, the more painful the adjustment period will be," Ito said. \par \par \uc2  Currently, Ito is a professor of economics at the University of Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Alastair Gale, Arran Scott and Bennett Richardson \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  (This item originally ran earlier.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in recent days to fight the soaring yen and may seek approval for its efforts from the U.S. authorities at the upcoming Group of Eight meeting, a former Finance Ministry official said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It's natural" for Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa to raise the issue when he meets U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow this weekend, said Takatoshi Ito, a former deputy vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  Ito said Shiokawa will likely sound out Snow to find out where he stands on Tokyo's struggle against the resurgent yen. Snow triggered a collapse in the dollar this week with remarks that traders say seemed to suggest he was wavering on the long-standing strong-dollar policy and with an apparent jab at Tokyo for its strong-arm tactics on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "As political pressure (on Japan), I'm not sure how serious (Snow's remarks were) meant to be. That should be found out," Ito told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not sure that that (Snow's comment) was really a prepared party line of the U.S. government." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said Wednesday he wouldn't bring up the topic of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Snow when the two meet on the sidelines of the G8 meeting in Deauville, France. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments will likely force Shiokawa's hand. The dollar plunged to Y115.56 overnight, its lowest level since last July. At 0830 GMT Thursday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y115.84. \par \par \uc2  Ito said a further rise in the yen may be quite damaging to Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  "That's the reason that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite heavily in recent days," Ito said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030515dz5f000lj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
