\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Economic Woes Roll On \b0\par\par\uc2 3,131 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2003\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Treasury Yields Slide As Stocks Suffer\par DJIA          8647.82    loss 31.43     dn 0.4%\par NASDAQ        1134.90    loss 4.78      dn 0.3%\par S&P 500       939.28     loss 3.02      dn 0.3%\par Dow Future    8640.00    loss 8.00      dn 0.1%\par NASDAQ Future 1152.50    gain 1.50      up 0.1%\par S&P Future    939.75     loss 0.50      dn 0.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.51% dn 0.11\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- A disappointing retail sales report sent Wall Street lower Wednesday after investors worried that the economic recovery might be sluggish in the months ahead. The same concern propelled Treasury bond prices higher. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Analysts said investors opted to cash in profits after pushing stocks higher for several weeks on better-than-expected earnings. Many are now looking for signs of continuing economic growth and the retail report failed to provide that, they said. \par \par \uc2  "People want a reason to be bullish, and unfortunately we just don't have the compelling evidence to have a sustainable new trend here," said Charles White, portfolio strategist at Avatar Associates. \par \par \uc2  "That said, we're probably a couple of good pieces of news on the economy away to be able to find a catalyst to break us out" much higher, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Commerce Department reported April sales at the nation's retailers slipped 0.1%, compared to a 2.3% gain in the previous month and forecasts of a 0.4% rise. Excluding sales at gasoline stations, retail sales went up by a modest 0.4% in April. \par \par \uc2  "We feel like the market will be stuck pretty much in a trading range for the rest of the year," with the upper limit of the S&P 500 around 960, said John Caldwell, chief equity strategist for McDonald Financial Group, part of Cleveland-based KeyCorp. \par \par \uc2  "What we worry about is folks getting too optimistic," he added. "Cautious optimism is our outlook for the year, because we do see better days ahead. We're worried folks may be getting a little too ahead of us." \par \par \uc2  BONDS: The benchmark 10-year note yield sunk to the lowest level in 45 years Wednesday, and the yield on the 30-year bonds also set a new record low. \par \par \uc2  The weak retail sales data added to the gloom about the economy and to disinflationary concerns first highlighted by Federal Reserve officials last week. As the session progressed, the bullishness in the market also brought in buyers who had been on the sidelines, reluctant to invest in bonds with such low yields. \par \par \uc2  "People don't want to be buying but feel they want to own Treasurys," said Brian Edmonds, head of Treasury trading at Banc of America Securities in New York. "We are being overwhelmed by the need for securities, especially the intermediate part of the curve." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Fall On Govt Plan Dismay\par USD-Yen             116.16       loss  0.17    dn 0.1%\par AUD-USD             0.6438       loss  0.0032  dn 0.5%\par Nikkei 225          8106.21      loss  138.70  dn 1.7%\par Hang Seng           9128.71      gain  25.02   up 0.3%\par JGB Yield           0.5850% up 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks declined Thursday, despite the\par Japanese government's release the day before of emergency measures to\par lift the stock market from near 20-year lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stock investors took their cue from a fall on Wall Street Wednesday,\par selling technology stalwarts Advantest, Fujitsu, NEC and Nippon\par Telegraph and Telephone as well as blue chip automakers Toyota, Honda\par and Nissan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The sell-off, which came amid corporate earnings announcements, was\par also a vote of no-confidence for Japan's latest plan to buoy stocks,\par traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late Wednesday, the government's top economic panel outlined\par stock-boosting measures. These included urging the postal services\par corporation - which manages 360 trillion yen in savings and insurance\par funds - to invest in a government-run stock fund. Another idea called\par for temporarily suspending public sales of stakes in Japan Tobacco Inc.\par and NTT to avoid flooding the market with new shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some proposals investors had wanted, such as revisions to Japan's\par securities and capital gains tax laws, were dropped. Critics said the\par plan fails to address the problems that have prevented the economy from\par shaking off a decade-long funk.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar's continued weakness also sparked selling of\par export-dependent shares, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets have wondered about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese\par financial authorities. Many traders have speculated that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  covertly bought dollars through Japanese banks to deflate the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro retreated from near four-year highs against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Will Euro-Scleroris Strike Again?\par Euro-USD            1.1446      loss  0.0042  dn 0.4%\par Stlg-USD            1.6140      loss  0.0034  dn 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.3207      gain  0.0035  up 0.3%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are expected to open slightly\par lower, with government bonds slightly higher in price and the euro\par fairly stable.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to open slightly lower as\par investors continue to debate whether recent market momentum can be\par sustained.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors are awaiting a barrage of corporate earnings reports,\par including such heavyweights as Allianz and ING. Deutsche Telecom earlier\par Thursday reported positive earnings and guidance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rolf Elgeti, strategist at Commerzbank, said the bear market is over,\par but the new bull market has yet to started. "We have moved to a neutral\par stance on equities as we believe the bear-market rally has happened and\par that the market is now at a crossroads," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Abhijit Chakrabortti, strategist at J.P. Morgan Global Equity, said\par the rally in European markets has run its course. He sees the\par strengthening euro as a cause for concern and believes the euro impact\par will force earnings downgrades for European companies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are expected to opens slightly\par higher on more economic misery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par German gross domestic product shrank in the first quarter of the year,\par German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said Wednesday in Hanover, Germany.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The finance minister said there was a "small minus" in GDP in the\par first quarter. His comments come ahead of the official release of\par figures from the Federal Statistics Office Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had forecast GDP growth of\par 0.2% in the first quarter, slightly up from the fourth quarter when the\par government reported zero growth after seasonal adjustments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government expects 0.75% growth this year, after 0.2% growth in\par 2002, and 0.6% growth the year earlier.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro continues to post small losses versus the dollar, but\par opens this session fairly steady around $1.1450.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The wave of profit-taking that has kept the dollar steady against the\par euro apparently originated in the euro/yen cross and ultimately placed\par pressure on the dollar/yen pair, analysts said. A growing sense that the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will cut interest rates at its meeting on June 5\par is reducing the euro's attraction. In recent weeks, the euro, supported\par by the ECB's benchmark rate being at 2.50%, has raced ahead of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from countries that offer lower interest rates, such as the\par U.S. and Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People are becoming more and more convinced that the ECB is going to\par cut rates and that's taking some of the froth off the appreciation the\par euro," says David Durrant, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with Bank Julius\par Baer in New York. "We're playing more off what the ECB is going to be\par doing and less off the U.S. data."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the U.K. Treasury denied a report by the BBC Wednesday that\par said Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown had ruled out U.K.\par adoption of the euro. "They've gone pretty hard on this, but they're\par just speculating," a Treasury spokesman said. "The policy stands that we\par will make the assessment by the first week of June."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Thursday, May 15: EU GDP, US Prices, Inventories\par ET/GMT\par 0200/0600 Germany      1Q GDP, seas. adj.(seen +0.2% QQ)\par 0245/0645 France       April Industrial Investment\par 0400/0800 Italy        1Q GDP (seen -0.1% QQ, +0.5% YY)\par 0530/0930 U.K.         Bank of England Inflation Report\par 0600/1000 E.U.         1Q GDP (seen +0.2 QQ, +1.1% YY)\par 0600/1000 E.U.         2Q GDP forecast\par 0800/1200 U.S.         April New York Fed Manufacturing Survey\par 0830/1230 U.S.         April Producer Price Index (seen -0.8%)\par 0830/1230 U.S.         Initial Jobless Claims for May 10 Week\par 0830/1230 U.S.         March Business Inventories (seen +0.2%)\par 0915/1315 U.S.         April Capacity Utilization (seen 74.4%)\par 0915/1315 U.S.         April Industrial Production\par 1000/1400 U.S.         DJ-BTM Business Barometer (last unch)\par 1000/1400 Germany      Bundesbank President Welteke speaks\par 1030/1430 U.S.         Treasury Undersecretary Taylor\par 1200/1600 U.S.         May Philadelphia Fed Business Index\par 1300/1700 U.S.         May Homebuilders' Housing Index\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Allianz: (AZ): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 7 analysts): EUR426M (EUR1.93B profit)\par Consensus sales: EUR14.5B (EUR13.7B)\par Note: Main problem seen writedowns on capital investments of around\par EUR800M - already announced - and restructuring costs for Dresdner\par Bank. Most analysts expect 1Q loss from Dresdner, due to loan-loss\par provisions of around EUR400M, while a few wouldn't rule out a small\par profit from ordinary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Still, Allianz's core insurance\par business is improving. Allianz already announced combined ratio below\par 100% for 1Q P&C. Analysts will focus on outlook for write-downs,\par loan-loss provisions, strategy for getting Dresdner Bank out of the\par red and further restructuring.\par Deutsche Telekom (DT): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 11 analysts): EUR487M (EUR1.81B)\par Consensus sales: EUR13.75B (EUR12.65B)\par Note: EBITDA to rise 12% to EUR4.24B. Debt expected to fall to\par EUR57.68B from EUR61.1B at end '02. Analysts focus on wireless unit,\par with scrutiny of domestic mobile margins and performance of\par T-MobileUSA. Also look for signs cost-cuts biting in fixed-line\par division and that the debt plan's on track.\par E.On (EON): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 8 analysts): EUR1.27B (EUR3.21B)\par Note:\par Consensus sales: EUR13.49B (EUR14.37B)\par Note:Operating profit seen up 8.5% at EUR1.40B. Figures for Ruhrgas,\par consolidated since Feb, will be main focus. Company expected to update\par '03 outlook including gas giant but give no news on synergies and\par details of integration. Analysts also see goal of US acquisition\par receding.\par ThyssenKrupp (G.TKP): 2Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 13 analysts): EUR121M (EUR66M)\par Consensus sales: EUR9.31B (EUR9.02B)\par Note: Pre-tax profit seen doubling to EUR191 million from EUR96M.\par Market not expecting forecast for full-year earnings, but will look\par for guidance on steel prices, volumes in 2H. Fiscal 04 target of\par EUR1.5B pretax seen virtually unreachable, but TKP seen unlikely to\par back away from it just yet.\par Arcelor (L.ARC): 1Q Revenue\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 4 analysts): EUR199M (EUR24M loss)\par Consensus sales: EUR6.79B (EUR6.82B)\par Note: Rising steel prices and cost reductions expected to lift company\par out of the red.\par Vivendi Universal (V): 1Q Revenue\par Consensus sales (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR6.42M (EUR14.65M)\par Note: Telecoms, including French \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Cegetel and Maroc Telecom\par expected to post 8% and 3% growth respectively, says Aurel Leven. Weak\par spots still seen in music \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and at Canal Plus where revenue is\par seen declining 14.5% and 15% respectively.\par Linde (G.LIN): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 11 analysts): EUR23M (EUR20M)\par Consensus sales: EUR1.93B (EUR1.95B)\par Note: EBITA down 4.7% to EUR123M from EUR129M. Market looking for gas\par division's earnings to remain stable at robust levels and at profit\par development at forklift division.\par Bank of Ireland PLC (IRE): FY Results\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR1.2B (EUR1.12B)\par Note: Cost reductions in Ireland and UK, health of Irish economy and\par state of BoI's pension funds are seen as key issues, says Davy\par Stockbrokers. Goodbody says sales growth is likely to be a "key\par challenge," .\par Fraport (G.FRP): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 6 analysts): EUR18.3M (EUR18.1M)\par Forecast range: EUR10M - EUR27.1M\par Consensus sales: EUR415.3M (EUR401.7M)\par Note: Operating profit of EUR51.3M, up 2.6% from EUR50M in 1Q last\par year. Estimates ranged between EUR34M to EUR65M. Nothing new or\par surprising expected as company recently released full-year figures.\par April traffic figures will give clue on 2Q performance. Fraport not\par seen hit by war, SARS as hard as its airline customers.\par ING (N.ING): 1Q earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR174.6M (EUR1.14B)\par Note: With markets still uncertain, ING isn't expected to give a\par full-year outlook.\par EFG Eurobank Ergasias (I.EFG): 1Q earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 4 analysts): EUR51.4M (EUR67M)\par Note: Lower trading gains, higher provisions and operating expenses all\par to blame for poor showing. But net interest income seen up 13% to\par EUR190M, up y-o-y, but down q-o-q given slower retail lending.\par Earnings momentum seen more positive in 2Q especially given more\par healthy stock markets relative to last year.\par Coca-Cola HBC (CCH): 1Q earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR37.9M (EUR38.7M)\par Consensus sales: EUR872.5M (EUR866.2M)\par Note: EBITDA seen up 4.5% to EUR90.5M from EUR86.6M.  1Q always\par company's weakest and not considered indicative of FY performance.\par Revenue growth small on back of changes to timing of Easter holidays,\par bad winter weather and price increases in selected markets as well as\par civil unrest in Nigeria. But net loss seen reduced vs 02 on efficient\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cost control.\par Tiscali (I.TSI) 1Q Revenue\par Consensus sales (DJ, 6 analysts): EUR211.6M (EUR195M)\par Note: EBITDA seen at EUR11.08M vs EUR1M last year. Eyes on cash burn\par rate and guidance for '03.\par BOC Group (BOX): 2Q Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 4 analysts): GBP95.9M (GBP99.1M)\par Forecast range: GBP90.9M - GBP101M\par Note:Analysts looking for outlook for semi-conductor gas supplying\par division, BOC Edwards, and evidence of cost cutting coming through,\par especially at Process Gas Solutions unit. Wondering if BOC keeping up\par with peer, Air Liquide\par London Stock Exchange (U.LSE): FY Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 10 analysts): GBP88.1M (GBP78.8M)\par Consensus sales: GBP236.5M (GBP215.6M)\par Note:Merrill Lynch analyst Manus Costello says market likely to focus\par on ability of company to grow and question its heavy cash retention,\par with payout ratio under 20%. But downside limited by strong balance\par sheet and cash generation.\par Safeway (U.SFW): FY Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP330M (GBP355.2M)\par Note:Numis analyst Mark Hughes says that all eyes will be on current\par trade, which he sees as -1% like-for-like. "That'll have the most\par leverage on the share price." Hoping to see same-store sales for the\par year just ended of +1.5%. Will be shock in market if pretax profit\par dips below GBP300M. Sees GBP321M pretax profit for current year.\par E.biscom (I.EBS): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 10 analysts): EUR69M (EUR48.4M)\par Consensus sales: EUR100M (EUR65.2M)\par Note: Seen posting a EUR12M EBITDA profit vs an EBITDA loss of EUR22M,\par as it was anticipated by the company a month ago. E.biscom will also\par post an operating loss of EUR56M according to the figures provided by\par the company's investor relations. Eyes on E.biscom net debt.\par MOL (R.MOL): 1Q Earnings, press briefing at 0900 GMT\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 7 analysts): HUF19B (HUF10.2B)\par Note: Chemicals division seen turning profitable but natural gas\par division remains in the red. Operating profit seen nearly tripling to\par HUF30.22B from HUF12.2B but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses on EUR debt and\par probable losses on hedging due to the weakness of USD will erode that.\par Danubius (R.DNB): 1Q Earnings\par Consensus pretax loss (DJ, 5 analysts): HUF1.86B (HUF1.13B)\par Consensus sales: HUF6.11B (HUF5.51B)\par Note: Tourism is usually the weakest in 1Q and company already warned\par it sees 03 remaining flat on 02. Sales up on consolidation of Slovak\par hotels but costs rise in tandem with sales and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses\par on EUR debt seen eating into revenues.\par Capital Radio (U.CAD): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP11.7M (GBP14.2M)\par Forecast range: GBP11.5M - GBP11.9M\par Note:Like-for-like revenues seen -5% as forecast in March trading\par update. Following recent weak audience data at core stations, update\par on program investment and promotion strategy and outlook for\par advertising market seen key.\par Hennes & Mauritz AB (S.HEM): April sales\par Note: Company seen posting Apr sales rise of 15% y/y, according to\par SME-Direkt survey of 14 analysts. Estimates in 11%-20% range. Helped\par by Easter shopping and warmer weather, growth seen up from Mar, when\par bad weather hampered growth in some key markets and sales grew 8%,\par below company's 10%-15% a year target. Last April sales rose 12%. H&M\par doesn't give actual monthly sales figures, only percentage change.\par 3i Group (U.TIG)\par Note: Seen posting full-year NAV at 459p a share, down 29% from 645p\par last year, according to a consensus of 3 analysts polled by Dow Jones\par Ne