\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar holds recent gains vs euro as bears rest. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 14 (Reuters) - The dollar held on to the previous session's gains on the euro on Wednesday after pulling off from four-year lows, drawing support from market fears about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Investors were also looking at fresh comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, who reaffirmed his administration's "strong dollar" policy after sparking bouts of dollar-selling earlier this week by casting doubts over it. \par \par \uc2  However, markets are still unconvinced about Washington's desire for a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of weak economic prospects in the United States. And analysts expect the euro to resume its rise against the dollar soon. \par \par \uc2  "With euro/dollar approaching all-time highs we may need a bit of a correction before we get there," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that Snow's comments failed to move euro/dollar higher means that we are a bit overdone for now...While Snow reaffirms his commitment to a "strong dollar" policy, it is a veiled sign that he is happy with current rates." \par \par \uc2  At 0848 GMT, the dollar was slightly up against the euro from the previous session at $1.1506 , after gaining more than a cent from the latest four-year lows of $1.1623 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was slightly down on the day at 116.68 , and close to its lowest level versus the Japanese unit since August 2002. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen hugged tight trading ranges with markets apprehensive about testing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market after traders detected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling yen for dollars in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied it was seeking a weaker yen to help exporters and said it only wanted to prevent excessive market moves. \par \par \uc2  But markets were nevertheless edgy after Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda issued a fresh warning the government should act "decisively" against rapid moves in the market. \par \par \uc2  RATES AND ECONOMY IN FOCUS \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also helped a bit by comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) Governing Council member Ernst Welteke who said the bank could have room for interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  Markets widely expect the ECB to trim interest rates, which at their relatively high level are helping boost the euro, in June. \par \par \uc2  "Welteke's comments set off an adjustment in the euro," said Ken Fujii, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Trust and Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. economic outlook is also on the front-burner with markets looking to a series of indicators due later this week. \par \par \uc2  Retail sales data for April is due at 1230 GMT. Economists surveyed by Reuters expect sales to have risen 0.4 percent, or 0.1 percent excluding autos, in April. That would be slower than gains of 2.1 percent, or 1.2 percent excluding autos, in March. \par \par \uc2  That data will be followed by producer prices inflation, industrial production and the Philadelphia Fed's survey of mid-Atlantic manufacturing activity, due on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030514dz5e00hae\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
