\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS-Stocks subdued, dollar steady after Saudi attacks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Bill Tarrant \par \uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 05:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 14 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied and Asian stocks were subdued on Wednesday after deadly bombing attacks in Saudi Arabia prompted a shift to save havens and pushed oil prices higher. \par \par \uc2  The dollar recovered from a four-year low against the euro after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow made clear the administration's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "We have a strong dollar policy and we've had it forever," he told Congress. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil prices held steady in Asian trading after soaring in New York following suicide bombings that killed at least 29 people in expatriate housing compounds in the Saudi capital. Washington blamed the attacks on al Qaeda. \par \par \uc2  Asian stock markets were generally flat after a technology-led rally on Wall Street faltered on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese stocks rose, led by gains in Sony Corp, after the consumer electronics giant said it would launch a handheld gaming device to take aim at Nintendo Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Sony climbed three percent, helping the Nikkei average gain 0.35 percent to 8,218.69 by the midsession break. The TOPIX index climbed 0.34 percent to 832.03. \par \par \uc2  Analysts saw upside in the market, saying banks and companies were unlikely to sell cross-held shares as the earnings season drew to a close. \par \par \uc2  "The supply and demand balance is improving. We should move even higher in the near term,' said Kazuyuki Naito, general manager of equities sales and trading at UFJ Tsubasa Securities. \par \par \uc2  SARS LIMITS GAINS \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks were up 0.76 percent, despite the territory's main carrier Cathay Pacific Airways falling 1.55 percent after it said April passenger traffic fell by 66 percent due to the SARS outbreak. \par \par \uc2  Singapore shares were down 0.72 percent after the battle to contain Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) took a turn for the worse when 27 people were isolated, raising fears of a new outbreak. \par \par \uc2  South Korea shares were off 0.6 percent, unsettled by a truckers strike that has hit the country's biggest port. \par \par \uc2  But SK Telecom was up 1.44 percent, after Nokia and Sony both announced plans for hybrid phone and game devices, giving a boost to the flagging telecom sector. \par \par \uc2  Australian shares were off 0.23 percent, despite New Corp gaining 2.1 percent after posting a strong third quarter and upping its year earnings guidance. Taiwan added 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Saudi explosions reminded Wall Street that U.S. geopolitical concerns extend far beyond Iraq. The stock sell-off came just a day after the broad Standard & Poor's 500 index scored its highest close since August. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq Composite Index dropped 0.11 percent to 1,539.68, a day after the tech-packed index hit its highest close since June. The blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial Average lost 0.54 percent to 8,679.25 and the broad S&P 500 slipped O.3 percent to 942.30. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  FEARS \par \par \uc2  The dollar recovered slightly from its recent lows against the yen as investors sensed that monetary authorities in Tokyo could step into the market at any time to try to weaken Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detected selling yen after anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments by Snow knocked the dollar down as low as 116.36 yen in late Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 116.77 yen at 0230 GMT, up slightly from 116.62 in late New York trade. The euro was trading around $1.1500, weakening from its late U.S. level of $1.1534. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro was expected to take a breather after its recent rally on speculation the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) may cut interest rates soon. \par \par \uc2  ECB board member Ernst Welteke said on Tuesday the bank had room to lower interest rates if oil prices and the dollar stayed low for long enough. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil for June delivery stood at $28.50 per barrel in electronic trading in Asia at 0230 GMT, around a three-week high, unchanged after a rise of $1.15 in New York. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was also holding steady in Asia, trading around $349.40 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  In the bond market, U.S. Treasuries were flat in Asia with traders reluctant to bring down yields already near four-decade lows. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030514dz5e005vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
