\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar off lows versus yen, seen vulnerable. \b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2003\par 01:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 15 (Reuters) - The dollar held some distance above its latest 10-month low versus the yen in early trade on Thursday but is likely to stay under pressure, with players keen to test Japan's resolve to curb the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar fell to around 115.60 yen, its lowest since July, after poor U.S. retail sales data for April, which indicated that a pillar of the U.S. economy might be showing some fatigue despite the end of hostilities in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Retail sales fell 0.1 percent in April compared with a forecast for a 0.4 percent increase. Sales excluding autos fell 0.9 percent, the biggest drop since September 2001. \par \par \uc2  As of 2315 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 116.17/22 yen slightly weaker than the 116.30 seen in late U.S. trade but still off Wednesday's low around 115.60. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar might be supported in Asia, because of wariness about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president at UFJ Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group. \par \par \uc2  "But it could slip again in post-Asian trade as some players think Japan has changed its stance to smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than blocking the yen's rise," he added. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may scale back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spread this week after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow made critical remarks about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an interview with Reuters. But some operators say the Japanese authorities remain determined to stop any yen rise above 115 yen per dollar, a level seen as the break-even point for many Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they have changed their stance at all. I think they will act when they want to," said Sahara of UFJ Bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1471/76 little changed from around $1.1480 in late U.S. trade. Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 133.26/37 yen, compared to 133.52 yen \par \par \uc2  The euro has lost momentum since it hit a four-year high of $1.1624 earlier this week on speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) might cut rates as the euro's strength is drawing complaints from European firms and government officials. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, French Finance Minister Francis Mer chimed in, saying the euro's rise against the dollar was "not very favourable in terms of (European) growth" and that there was room for movement on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  ECB Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said on Tuesday the bank could have room for rate cuts, if oil prices and the dollar stayed low. \par \par \uc2  The market is keenly awaiting a series of U.S. economic indicators due on Thursday, including producer prices, industrial production and the Philadelphia Fed's survey of mid-Atlantic manufacturing activity. \par \par \uc2  Weak figures could raise worries about deflation in the U.S. economy - stoked after the Fed warned of the risk of falls in prices earlier this month - and could accelerate dollar selling, dealers said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030514dz5e017iq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
