\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Euro rally could bring Made in Japan problems home. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 15:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 14 (Reuters) - The euro's breakneck rally to four-year peaks on the dollar and yen is bringing fat profits to overseas investors in the euro zone but risks creating Japanese-style economic troubles. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen nearly 10 percent against the dollar this year alone mainly because investors have poured money into liquid, higher yielding euro zone government bonds as an alternative to dollar assets, and because the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept interest rates on hold. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the region now has bigger risks of slower growth and deflation - a problem familiar to Japan, where a four-year-long decline in consumer prices, a decade of sluggish growth and an appreciating yen threaten to damage its fragile export-driven recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Capital gains from weakening growth and the euro rally are putting euro zone bonds into self-reinforcing profitability. The strength of the euro tightens the economy so bonds rally even further and the economy goes into trouble," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Bank of Scotland Treasury Services. \par \par \uc2  "It's a yen paradox - the euro zone's parallels with Japan are a swing into current account surplus due to weak domestic demand and depressed import levels, strong demand for government bonds in a disinflationary or deflationary environment and an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  compounding the problem." \par \par \uc2  In 2002, the euro zone had its first current account surplus since the euro's introduction, with a surplus of 62.0 billion euros ($71.29 billion). Japan's 2002 current account surplus stood at 13.3 trillion yen ($114 billion). \par \par \uc2  "Underperformance of the euro zone economy suggests outperformance of bonds there... Japan and the euro zone both run a current account surplus so an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be harmful," said Steven Saywell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  MADE IN JAPAN? \par \par \uc2  After Japan's asset bubble burst in 1990, its massive current account surplus drove the yen to a record high beyond 80 yen to the dollar in 1995 from 160 earlier. Tokyo stocks  more than halved in the same period, having hit an all-time high in 1989. HBOS estimates nominal GDP growth also plunged from 10 percent year-on-year in early 1990 to flat by March 1995. \par \par \uc2  For the euro zone, Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley, estimates every 10 percent rise in the average euro/dollar rate year on year leads to a fall of around one percent in GDP over two years. \par \par \uc2  "Our forecast for euro zone GDP is only 0.9 percent. Growth can be easily wiped away by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," Jen said. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB is way behind the curve so the yield curve will flatten, bringing capital gains for the long end. The dollar is structurally sick. Global capital has nowhere to go but to the euro. The euro will keep rallying but that will put Europe in trouble." \par \par \uc2  HAPPY SO FAR, BUT... \par \par \uc2  Euro zone finance ministers and ECB officials seem happy so far to see the euro, once 30 percent below its 1999 launch rate, finally recognised as a true rival to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Nikos Christodoulakis, Greece's finance minister and current chairman of the Eurogroup, said the surging euro helps contain inflationary pressures and strengthens consumer buying power. \par \par \uc2  However, Jen said euro zone consumers' perception of inflation remained high due to domestic price hikes when euro notes and coins were introduced last year and this discouraged them from spending. \par \par \uc2  "The shock to the euro zone economy from an overshoot in the euro will be very severe. To prevent this, the obvious thing (the ECB) can do is to cut interest rates. Then they can perhaps initiate verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  like \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been doing, followed by actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing." \par \par \uc2  So what would be the worst-case scenario for the euro zone? \par \par \uc2  "U.S. business confidence fails to recover, equities slump and the dollar starts to fall sharply, euro zone growth underperforms because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact, causing further inflows into European government bonds and a further widening in the euro zone current account surplus," Pearson said. \par \par \uc2  "Both of which would create additional euro demand and propel euro/dollar to $1.30-$1.40. \par \par \uc2  "This would create a similar overvaluation of the euro relative to purchasing power parity to the dollar/yen axis and necessitate regular ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength from exacerbating a disinflationary spiral." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030514dz5e00r0c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
