\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Volatilities Mixed In Choppy Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 18:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were mixed Wednesday in New York, as the yen rallied against the dollar and the euro, and a conviction that a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut was in the future shored up the dollar against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Early Wednesday, the yen rapidly appreciated against the dollar and the euro, raising the specter that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may use the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally to devalue the yen against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro and the dollar fell to their lowest levels against the yen since last Thursday, dipping to Y133.32 and Y115.56, respectively. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-yen has a great deal of catching up to do in terms of dollar weakness," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital Trading in Warren, N.J. "The market's worried that any break through Y115.50 could induce huge dollar selling." \par \par \uc2  Strategists believe that any break below Y116 would be  tough for the Japanese to unilaterally hold back, and that breaking such a level could lead to the dollar trading at Y110 by the end of May and possibly Y102 by the end of the summer. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Leaver said, the likelihood was more for small-scale covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of the Japanese, rather than massive across-the-board yen selling. \par \par \uc2  Around noon EDT (1600 GMT), the dollar was trading at Y116.13, down from Y116.60 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1510, down from $1.1513 late Tuesday, but off its session lows of $1.1471. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at $1.3138, down from CHF1.3140 late Tuesday but up from CHF1.3120 earlier in the session. Sterling was at $1.6165, up compared with $1.6091. \par \par \uc2  Helping to stabilize the dollar against the euro was growing sentiment that the ECB's benchmark interest rate of 2.50% couldn't be maintained forever, an idea reinforced by recent comments from European finance officials. Echoing earlier comments by ECB member and Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, French Finance Minister Francis Mer told reporters that the euro's rise, which makes euro-zone exports less competitive on global markets, "is not very favorable to economic growth." \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 10.10%-10.40%, up from 9.60%-9.75% earlier in Tokyo. One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 10.50%-10.80%, down from 10.70%-10.95% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor dollar puts at -1.70% to -1.30%. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls are favored against the dollar by 0.50%-0.90%. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248; michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Corrected May 14, 2003 22:13 ET (02:13 GMT) 