\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Dips Below Y116 As Traders Test BOJ Resolve \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 18:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Michael Deibert and Robert Flint \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar dipped briefly below Y116 Wednesday afternoon as traders appeared to test the resolve of Japanese authorities to slow the yen's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  After hitting a new 10-month low around Y115.62, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced almost immediately to Y116.00, well off its late Tuesday level of Y116.60. Around 1:20 pm EDT, the dollar was back at Y115.90. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rapid rise against the dollar and the euro has been the focus of the New York session. The euro dropped as low Y133.11, before retracing back to Y133.41. \par \par \uc2  The yen's surge has raised the specter of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  using the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. Traders are convinced the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly earlier this week and last week, channeling funds through Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  The gains in the yen come after U.S Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated Tuesday that governments ought not to try to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Analysts are wondering whether the MOF might be hesitant to act given the U.S.'s apparent opposition to efforts to slow the yen's rise at the dollar's expense, particularly ahead of this weekend's meeting of Group of Eight finance ministers in Deauville, France. \par \par \uc2  "We feel there is far less likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hours before Snow and (Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro) Shiokawa meet face to face," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust. "We do however expect some verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tonight, with Japanese officials reiterating that rapid moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not desirable," he added. \par \par \uc2  However, earlier Wednesday, Shiokawa said that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be on the agenda when he holds talks with Snow Friday in France. \par \par \uc2  Partly, Wednesday's sharp move reflects the fact that  the dollar-yen pair hasn't moved as sharply as the euro-dollar pair, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-yen has a great deal of catching up to do in terms of dollar weakness," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital Trading in Warren, NJ. "The market's worried that any break through Y115.50 could induce huge dollar selling." \par \par \uc2  Strategists believe that any sustained break below Y116 would make it tough for the Japanese to unilaterally hold back, and that breaking such a level could lead to the dollar trading at Y110 by the end of May. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Leaver said, the likelihood was more for small-scale covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of the Japanese, rather than massive across-the-board yen selling. \par \par \uc2  After struggling for about 20 minutes to regain the Y116 level, the dollar suddenly shot to Y116.20, strongly suggesting to some traders that covert Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had come to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rescue. \par \par \uc2  However, traders also noted that there is no firm evidence that the BOJ is acting through Japanese banks and that the spurt of volatility could simply be a reflection of a nervous market. Around 1.30 p.m. EDT (1730 GMT), the dollar was at Y116.10, with the euro at Y133.55. \par \par \uc2  -By Robert Flint and Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, 1 201 938 4408, robert.flint@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030514dz5e009ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
