\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rescued by Japan buying after slide on Snow. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2003\par 13:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 13 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied above recent lows against the yen and the euro on Tuesday, aided by the Bank of Japan, after fresh comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow sent the greenback reeling in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they saw the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars for yen after the greenback fell towards last week's 10-month low. In common with recent practice, there was no confirmation from the Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Snow said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be determined in the open market and that Japan and Europe should not blame a weaker dollar for their economic woes. Snow's interview was conducted on Friday but embargoed until Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Rhetoric like that suggests there is no major restriction coming from the U.S. for investors to sell the dollar. But the message from Japan is clear: they don't want to see the yen strengthening," said Osman Wahid, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was holding gains of half a percent on the day at $1.1510\par per euro  and was steady at 116.80 yen        by the European\par midsession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A weekend remark from Snow that a weaker dollar would help U.S. exports\par knocked the dollar beyond $1.16 to the euro on Monday for the first\par time in over four years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Snow speaks again at 2000 GMT on the state of the international\par financial system and the International Monetary Fund.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STRAINED RELATIONS?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the timing of Snow's comments was significant ahead of a\par meeting of G8 finance ministers in France this weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The U.S. seems to be saying that they don't have a problem with dollar\par weakness and Europe and Japan shouldn't either," said Michael Metcalfe,\par senior strategist at State Street.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, dealers saw little sign that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would give up its policy of\par covert \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent the yen's strength against the dollar\par harming the country's exports - the one bright spot in Japan's\par economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan admitted spending nearly 2.5 trillion yen from January to March\par to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and help exporters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders suspected this policy of covert \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  resumed late last\par week after a pause in April. The research arm of Japanese money\par brokerage Totan Research said the Bank of Japan was estimated to have\par sold as much as 1.9 trillion yen over the past three business days.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. trade data due later on Tuesday may be a potential negative factor\par for the dollar, with the figures expected to show the trade gap widened\par slightly in March to $40.75 billion from $40.32 billion in February.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Europe, the data highlight was the ZEW institute's expectations\par indicator for the German economy which rose for a fifth straight month\par in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030513dz5d00n7k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
