\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-BOJ seen selling yen after Snow remarks hurt dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2003\par 12:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detected conducting yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday after comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow had knocked the dollar down. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Bank of Japan (BOJ), acting as agent for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was seen repeatedly buying the dollar after it fell as low as 116.36 yen  in late Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  In common with recent practice, there was no confirmation from the Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  The action came as the dollar nosedived towards its recent low of 116 yen after Snow expressed disapproval of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and "devaluation strategies" in particular, as a way of helping an economy, saying Japan and Europe should not blame a weaker dollar for their economic woes. \par \par \uc2  By 0920 GMT the dollar had recovered sharply to 117.25 yen, up nearly a yen from the day's low of 116.36 yen. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to Reuters in an interview conducted on Friday but embargoed by the Treasury until Tuesday, Snow said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be kept "to a minimum". \par \par \uc2  "I don't want to be critical of other countries but our broad policy is that we prefer to see relative values of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... determined through open exchange markets," Snow said. \par \par \uc2  His comments sparked speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent a strong yen hurting exports may become more difficult diplomatically for Japan. \par \par \uc2  But, far from taking heed of Snow's remarks, Japanese officials apparently moved instead to repair what they saw as the damage done. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have made no secret of their dissatisfaction with the yen's recent gains, although they have declined to comment on whether they have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently. \par \par \uc2  "Over the past two weeks (the dollar) has moved about five yen and this is a bit unnatural," Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told a news conference on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  "We need something like a warning," he said. \par \par \uc2  From January to March, Japan spent nearly 2.5 trillion yen ($21.37 billion at Tuesday's exchange rate) to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and help exporters. \par \par \uc2  The research arm of Japanese money brokerage Totan Research said the Bank of Japan was estimated to have sold as much as 1.9 trillion yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past three business days from Thursday, calculating from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's data on money market conditions. ($1=117.00 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030513dz5d00izk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
