\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar fights back, BoJ seen repairing Snow damage. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2003\par 10:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 13 (Reuters) - The dollar fought back from recent losses with the help of the Bank of Japan on Tuesday, after a fresh set of comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow sent it reeling. There was no confirmation from Japanese authorities that they had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but traders in Tokyo said they saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars for yen after the greenback was knocked a yen lower by Snow. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Snow attacked any efforts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe should not blame a weaker dollar for their economic woes. \par \par \uc2  Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Bank in London, said Snow was trying to head off any expectation that Washington would back European or Japanese action to stem their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, the message is: 'don't expect any protests over what's happening," Gunner said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had been rebounding from recent lows in Asian trade but dived against the euro and the yen when Snow's comments came out. It then recovered most of the day's preceding gains after the BoJ was seen in the market. \par \par \uc2  By 0818 GMT the dollar was still up half a percent from Monday's New York close at $1.1507 per euro, more than a cent above Monday's four-year low. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was a quarter percent higher on the day versus the yen at 117.11, having risen sharply from the Snow-induced lows around 116.37, the level at which traders said the BoJ had been seen starting its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the yen was a third of a percent up on the day at 134.81. \par \par \uc2  LONE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) declined comment on whether it had helped its Japanese counterpart stem the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ has come covertly into the markets on several occasions this year in a bid to smooth out the yen's rise against the battered dollar. \par \par \uc2  Snow made his comments to Reuters on Friday but they were embargoed until Tuesday. He had already sent the dollar to four-year lows against the euro on Monday after he said at weekend that its weakness was beneficial to U.S. exporters. \par \par \uc2  This time, he said devaluation strategies were not calculated to breed long-run domestic prosperity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be kept to a minimum. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, concerned that the yen's rise against the dollar could damage hopes for economic recovery, repeatedly say that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves should reflect fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Snow told Reuters however: "Their economies (Japan and euro zone) have had quite slow growth rates at various levels of the dollar and the Japanese economy has been in a very slow growth rate over a long period of time, during which the value of the dollar has fluctuated." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030513dz5d00gza\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
