\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Closes Mixed; Some Traders Expect More Weakening \b0\par\par\uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 01:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar split its performance Tuesday, slipping against the yen but\par gaining back some ground against the euro after gyrating in the wake of\par comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow and a negative U.S. trade\par report.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the dollar was trading at 116.60 yen, down from 116.99 yen late Monday in New York but off its Tuesday session lows of 116.36 yen. The euro was trading at $1.1513, down from $1.1544 late Monday. Sterling was at $1.6091 compared with $1.6083. \par \par \uc2  But many strategists feel the dollar is merely pausing on its way down and that yawning deficits will continue to pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against its rivals. \par \par \uc2  "It's been such a dramatic move down that the dollar will consolidate before weakening further," said Paresh Uphadyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Putnam Investments in Boston. "The question is, as the trade deficit continues to widen -- thereby increasing the current-account deficit -- how will the government finance it in the wake of the decrease of net foreign inflows into the U.S.?" \par \par \uc2  According to data released by the U.S. Commerce Department early Tuesday, the deficit in international trade for goods and services ranked in March as the second-worst single report ever. The deficit widened to $43.46 billion in March from a revised deficit of $40.37 billion in February, adjusted from $40.32 billion. The performance resulted in the first quarter having the widest quarterly deficit ever. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts are expecting the next numbers for the U.S. current-account deficit, of which the trade deficit is a substantial component, to run at about 5.5% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "In the context of the comments from Snow, [the trade deficit] is significant," said Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. "I think this just emphasizes the fact that there is a growing trade deficit despite the weak dollar." \par \par \uc2  In recent comments, Mr. Snow mentioned the dollar's lower exchange rate would help U.S. exports and implied the U.S. wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. However, he and the Bush administration have repeatedly stated the so-called strong dollar policy hasn't changed, even though many market participants believe it no longer really exists. \par \par \uc2  Underscoring the current jitters in the market surrounding official U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, the dollar was even momentarily lifted by a seemingly innocuous comment by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, who said, "The soundness of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is essential to the soundness of its economy." \par \par \uc2  His comments, delivered in the context of the U.S. stepping up its anti-counterfeiting efforts by unveiling a new $20 dollar bill, clearly didn't refer to anything to do with the dollar's level. Even so, some traders wondered for a fleeting moment if he had announced yet another change in U.S. dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was able to take some solace during the morning from comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member and Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke that the bank could lower euro-zone interest rates if oil prices and the euro remain at current levels, sending the euro below $1.1500 on speculation that the euro's high-yield advantage may not be a permanent feature. \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls also noted comments by the head of the International Monetary Fund urging market caution over the weakness of the U.S. dollar, saying he believed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would bounce back from current levels. \par \par \uc2  "Up to now, the decline of the dollar is still orderly," Horst Koehler, IMF managing director, told reporters in Geneva. "I think we should not exaggerate this development or even dramatize it." \par \par \uc2  Also putting a limit on the dollar's slide was the widely-held belief that the Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was continuing to covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Funneling the funds through Japanese banks, the BOJ spent as much as 1.2 trillion yen ($10.26 billion) last Thursday and Friday to stop the dollar from weakening against the yen. Covert sales of yen have continued this week, the same analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the New Zealand dollar managed to hit a new 5-year high against the dollar at US$0.5821 in overnight trading, taking advantage of the dollar's still relatively weak tone against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Australian dollar came within a whisker of hitting a fresh 40-month high above US$0.6503, buoyed by ongoing support from offshore buyers chasing Australia's high interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The slight firmness of the dollar Tuesday also helped push gold futures lower, as gold settled $1.70 lower at $350.20 per ounce Tuesday after the slightly steadier U.S. dollar prompted some light profit taking and book squaring in bullion. The continued strength of U.S. equity markets also served to lean on gold by distracting would-be investor interest away from the gold market. \par \par \uc2  Write to Michael Deibert at michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Gavin Maguire contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-13-03 1910ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030513dz5d00a6p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
