\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Consolidating But Likely Due For Further Slide \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2003\par 20:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Following a morning that saw the dollar retrace some of its overnight gains in the wake of comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow and a disastrous U.S. trade report, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is splitting its performance against its major rivals Tuesday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Compared with day-earlier levels, the dollar is slightly ahead versus the Swiss franc and euro, little changed versus sterling, but down against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Around 2:30 p.m. EDT (1830 GMT), the dollar is trading at Y116.53, down from Y116.99 late Monday in New York but off it's Tuesday session lows of Y116.36. The euro was trading at $1.1530 from $1.1544 late Monday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3135, up from CHF1.3109 late Monday. Sterling was at $1.6079 compared with $1.6083. \par \par \uc2  Many strategists feel that the dollar is merely pausing on its way down and that yawning deficits will continue to pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against its rivals. \par \par \uc2  "It's been such a dramatic move down that the dollar will consolidate before weakening further," said Paresh Uphadyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Putnam Investments in Boston. "The question is, as the trade deficit continues to widen - thereby increasing current account deficit - how will the government finance it in the wake of the decrease of net foreign inflows into the U.S.?" \par \par \uc2  The deficit in international trade in goods and services, ranking as the second worst single report and worst-ever quarter on record, widened to $43.46 billion in March from a revised deficit of $40.37 billion in February, adjusted from $40.32 billion. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts are expecting the next numbers for the U.S. current account deficit, of which the trade deficit is a substantial component, to run at about 5.50% of the U.S. gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "In the context of the comments from Snow, (the trade deficit) is significant," said Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. "I think this just emphasizes the fact that there is a growing trade deficit despite the weak dollar." \par \par \uc2  In a recent interviews, Snow mentioned that the dollar's lower exchange rate would help U.S. exports and implied the U.S. wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stop the dollar's decline. However, Snow and the Bush administration have repeatedly stated the so-called strong dollar policy hasn't been changed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was able to take some solace during the morning on comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member and Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke that the bank could lower euro-zone interest rates if oil prices and the euro remain at current levels, sending the euro below $1.1500 on speculation that the euro's high-yield advantage may not be a permanent feature. \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls also noted comments by the head of the International Monetary Fund urging market caution over the weakness of U.S. dollar, saying he believed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would bounce back from current levels. \par \par \uc2  "Up to now, the decline of the dollar is still orderly," Horst Koehler, IMF managing director, told reporters in Geneva on Tuesday. "I think we should not exaggerate this development or even dramatize it." \par \par \uc2  Also putting a limit on the dollar's slide was the widely-held belief that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was continuing to covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the dollar against the yen. Funneling the funds through Japanese banks, the BOJ spent as much as Y1.2 trillion last Thursday and Friday to stop the dollar from weakening against the yen. Covert sales of yen have continued this week, the same analysts said. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, \par \par \uc2  201-938-2248, email:michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030513dz5d001ah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
