\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : "Snow Squeeze" Puts End To Dlr Shortcovering \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Twaronite \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2003\par 08:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- An early dollar rise Tuesday as market participants took profits on short positions suddenly reversed course as fresh comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below Monday's late New York levels. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters interview quoted Snow as saying that Japan and Europe shouldn't blame a weaker dollar for their economic woes, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would do little to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy in the long run. \par \par \uc2  "As I've said on various occasions, devaluation strategies are not well calculated to breed long-run domestic prosperity," Snow was quoted as saying. "You can't devalue your way to long-term high standards of living." \par \par \uc2  While Snow reaffirmed the U.S.' commitment to its strong dollar policy, his remarks seemed to cast aspersions on the Japanese government's policy of selling yen whenever it deemed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was appreciating too much. \par \par \uc2  Market participants have said Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been putting a floor under the dollar in recent sessions since last Thursday, quietly selling yen for dollars through Japanese banks in the mid-Y116s. \par \par \uc2  But the ministry showed no sign that it would take Snow's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks to heart. \par \par \uc2  "We consider (the reported Snow comments) not to be current news," Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Reuters said Snow made the remarks Friday but the U.S. Treasury embargoed them until today. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y116.48, below Y116.99 in New York and Y116.88 in Tokyo late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1543, a tick lower than $1.1544 in New York and below $1.1560 in Tokyo. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y134.45, compared with Y135.05 in New York and Y135.07 in Tokyo Monday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6087, compared with $1.6083 in New York and $1.6079 in Tokyo Monday. \par \par \uc2  Snow's remarks follow a spate of weekend comments in which he reaffirmed the U.S.' strong dollar policy but undermined his affirmations by stating that a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps U.S. exporters. \par \par \uc2  Before the afternoon Snow storm, trading was dominated by euro sales, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The price action in everything this morning was driven by the euro," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan. "The technical picture looks a little less bullish, with it failing to sustain highs." \par \par \uc2  At one point early in the session, profit-taking pulled the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below $1.1500 for the first time this week. \par \par \uc2  Market participants saw that $1.1600 was broken overnight, and "they thought, time for a correction," said a Japanese bank trader. \par \par \uc2  While many market participants remain bullish on the euro's longer term prospects, some are also concerned about economic fundamentals. The euro's rapid appreciation threatens eurozone growth by raising the specter of deflation as it cheapens imports. It also hurts European exporters by reducing the value of their overseas earnings when repatriated. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro also lost ground on profit-taking. Japanese exporters sold the euro above Y135 in the morning, and then Snow's comments pushed it decisively below Y135 in the afternoon to a low of Y134.20. \par \par \uc2  -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Alastair Gale \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030513dz5d000d5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
