\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:Dlr Drops; Mkt Takes Snow's Comments As Cue To Sell \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Twaronite \par \uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2003\par 04:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar got hammered against the euro and yen in Asia Monday morning, as traders took weekend comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow to mean Washington won't stand in the way of a weaker dollar. \par \par \uc2  In a speech on Friday and a television program on Sunday, Snow paid lip service to the U.S.' strong dollar stance. But on Sunday, he said a weaker dollar could help U.S. exports - which many market participants took as a clear green light to sell the dollar against the yen and euro. \par \par \uc2  "This is (Snow's) real intention," said Morgan Stanley's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Toru Umemoto, referring to Snow's remarks that a weaker dollar could promote U.S. exports. "It's a slip of tongue" that revealed what's really on Snow's mind, Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2  The remarks unleashed a torrent of dollar selling first thing Monday morning. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a session low of Y116.59 - down sharply from around Y117.23 late Friday in New York. The euro rose as high as $1.1579, up from $1.1493 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  At 0242 GMT Monday, the dollar was at Y116.91 and the euro was at $1.1573. \par \par \uc2  Traders also read between the lines of other remarks by Snow. And they found more dollar-sell signals. \par \par \uc2  Snow said the best way to combat a weak dollar is to ensure that U.S. economic fundamentals are strong. Many traders interpreted that caveat as a yellow caution light to anyone who thought of buying dollars on the reiteration of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "Snow said 'strong dollar, strong dollar,' but then he said the value of the dollar depends on fundamentals. That implied benign neglect," said Kaneo Ogino, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at HSBC in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  A weak U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives a tail wind to U.S. exporters by making their goods cheaper and therefore more appealing in overseas markets, and boosting the value of their earnings when repatriated. U.S. manufacturers, particularly in the automotive sector, have long lobbied that the U.S. Treasury abandon its commitment to a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par          Japan MOF Fighting Dollar Sell-Off\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ogino said Snow's strong-dollar comments may have been intended to\par tacitly support Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its efforts to combat yen\par strength.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Snow's remarks meant, "it's up to you, MOF, you can do whatever you\par want," as far as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Ogino said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders suspect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Ministry has been covertly selling yen\par this month to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise. It was widely rumored to have\par sold yen for dollars around Y117.10 on Friday and again Monday through\par two large Japanese banks, market participants said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If indeed the Finance Ministry acted this morning, it failed to\par prevent dollar sellers from triggering stop loss orders below Y117. But\par Japan's yen selling may have helped the dollar rise off of session lows,\par traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A top Japanese finance official kept traders' suspicions about\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alive Monday morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We're continuing to watch markets closely and we will take the most\par appropriate measures for the situation," said Zembei Mizoguchi, Vice\par Finance Minister for International Affairs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030512dz5c0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
