\par\par \b \uc2  Dealers certain Japan sold yen - Tokyo stays mum. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 10:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 9 (Reuters) - Tokyo officials kept the market guessing on Friday over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in recent sessions, but many dealers were convinced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had moved to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-damaging rise. After hitting a 10-month low around 116 yen in London trade on Thursday, the dollar has bounced above 117 yen, fuelling suspicions that Japanese authorities have been quietly selling yen in line with their new "stealth" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. "We are pretty sure that the Japanese monetary authorities have stepped in from between 117.00-117.20 yen in the morning, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically through selected number of banks," one senior trader in Tokyo told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "They've been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since London time (on Thursday) when the dollar hit a low of around 116 yen and then around 116.50 yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Although Japanese officials have made no secret of their displeasure about the yen's strength, their new policy of not immediately announcing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and putting a strict gag order on their counterparties has made it impossible to confirm. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said cryptic comments by Finance Ministry officials made them all the more wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "On whether we have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market...it is a very subtle subject and I cannot say anything about it," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "But we are not without interest." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's deputy, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, also declined to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but warned that it would act if needed. \par \par \uc2  "As always, we will take action if necessary depending on market conditions," Mizoguchi told reporters. \par \par \uc2  At 0732 GMT, the dollar stood at 117.27/32 yen up a full yen from Thursday's low of 116 yen. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had likely taken place because the dollar was approaching a technically crucial level around 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  "It's not surprising if they've done it, because if they don't do it now, the dollar will fall even more rapidly once the 115-yen barrier is broken," said another dealer. \par \par \uc2  STEALTH \par \par \uc2  The dollar broke its recent 118-121 yen range earlier this week and fell steeply after the U.S. Federal Reserve cautioned of a possible "unwelcome substantial fall in inflation" on Tuesday although it kept interest rates unchanged. The market's bearish view of the U.S. economy has weakened the dollar across the board, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to four-year lows against the euro. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' worries heightened after the dollar broke below 117 yen on Wednesday because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted its first "stealth" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January in the mid-117s. \par \par \uc2  Since January, Japanese monetary authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  discreetly in the hope that that would be more effective than the conventional method of stepping in in large amounts and immediately announcing their actions. \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministry data on Thursday showed Japanese authorities spent 2.38 trillion yen ($20.33 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from January to March, buying dollars and euros for yen. ($1=117.09 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030509dz5900fl6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
