\par\par \b \uc2  Euro hits life high against yen as ECB stands pat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 08:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 9 (Reuters) - The euro vaulted to a lifetime high versus the yen and hit four-year peaks versus the dollar on Friday as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep interest rates on hold maintained the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appeal as a yield earner. \par \par \uc2  The latest surge was also fuelled by ECB chief Wim Duisenberg's comment that there was nothing excessive about the current level of the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The trend shows no sign of stopping. The ECB doesn't seem to be alarmed by the euro's current levels," said Koichi Ono, economist at Daiwa Institute of Research. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as far as 135.26 yen its highest level since the introduction of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  The level was sharply higher from around 134.35 yen in late U.S. trade and 132 yen in late Asian trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro also benefited from concerns that Japanese officials might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen and by rumours that Japanese authorities may have already been in the market. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro rose as high as $1.1537 a fresh four-year high and up a half cent from around $1.1480 in late U.S. trade. Dealers said the next target was $1.16. \par \par \uc2  The euro opened at $1.1745 at its launch, and is expected to inch up towards that level. \par \par \uc2  The euro has been rising steadily since the beginning of April as global investors pour funds into the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to its higher-yielding edge over the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Europe's interest rates - the benchmark is 2.50 percent - are double the Federal Reserve's federal funds rate. \par \par \uc2  The euro took a breather on Thursday in the run-up to the ECB's rate-setting meeting but dealers were emboldened after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's decision came after the U.S. Fed earlier this week hinted at its readiness to cut rates, already at four-decade lows, citing the risk of an unwanted fall in inflation. \par \par \uc2  Junya Tanase, global markets officer at J.P. Morgan Chase, said: "Now that the Fed is on an easing bias and the ECB has kept its rate steady, I think the market is expecting a further widening of the yield gap between the dollar and the euro." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CONCERN \par \par \uc2  Thanks to the euro's rise against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was at 117.30/35 yen well above the overnight 10-month low of 116 yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen has gained about four percent against the dollar in two weeks, raising concern that its strength will make Japanese products more expensive abroad and dampen the country's exports. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also helped by concerns about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said the yen has strengthened too much but declined to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Rumours swirled among market participants that Japan may have stepped in overnight and in Tokyo on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's deputy, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, also declined to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but warned that it would act if needed. \par \par \uc2  "As always, we will take action if necessary depending on market conditions," Mizoguchi told reporters on his way to work. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar also drew some downside support as Japanese investors are expected to keep snapping up foreign bonds amid ever-falling domestic interest rates. The yield on 30-year JGBs fell below one percent to a record low 0.995 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030509dz5900ayo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
