\par\par \b \uc2  Euro matches life high vs yen, hits 4-yr peak on dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 04:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 9 (Reuters) - The euro rallied to match its life high against the yen and hit four-year peaks versus the dollar on Friday as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep interest rates on hold maintained the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appeal as a yield earner. \par \par \uc2  The latest surge was also fuelled by ECB chief Wim Duisenberg's comment that there was nothing excessive about the current level of the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The euro looks very strong," said Gen Kawabe, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  "Even if it falls, there's enough buying interest to push it back up and that doesn't apply to all higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Kawabe added. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as far as 135.13 yen, matching a record high hit shortly after the euro's launch in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro was meeting heavy resistance around that level, but that it would be only a matter of time before it broke that closely watched target. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro rose as high as $1.1535 - a fresh four-year high - in morning trade before standing at $1.1518/23 at 0215 GMT. Dealers said the next target was $1.16. \par \par \uc2  The euro opened at $1.1745 at its launch, and is expected to inch up towards that level. \par \par \uc2  The euro has been rising steadily since the beginning of April as global investors poured funds into the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to its higher-yielding edge over the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Europe's interest rates - the benchmark is 2.50 percent - are double the Federal Reserve's federal funds rate. \par \par \uc2  The euro took a breather on Thursday in the run-up to the ECB's rate-setting meeting but dealers were emboldened by news that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Junya Tanase, global markets officer at J.P. Morgan Chase, said: "Now that the Fed is on an easing bias and the ECB has kept its rate steady, I think the market is expecting a further widening of the yield gap between the dollar and the euro." \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR/YEN EDGY \par \par \uc2  Thanks to the euro's rise against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was at 117.30/35 yen, well above the overnight 10-month low of 116 yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen has gained about four percent against the dollar in two weeks, raising concern that its strength will make Japanese products more expensive abroad and dampen the country's exports. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also helped by concerns about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said the yen has strengthened too much but declined to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Rumours swirled among market participants that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight when the yen appreciated to 116 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's deputy, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, also declined to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but warned that it would act if needed. \par \par \uc2  "As always, we will take action if necessary depending on market conditions," Mizoguchi told reporters on his way to work. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar also drew some downside support as Japanese investors are expected to keep snapping up foreign bonds amid the yen's rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors are desperate for higher-yielding assets as Japan's interest rates have fallen to record low levels. \par \par \uc2  But dollar/yen moves were more subdued than the euro as the market suffered a dearth of incentives with fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dampening sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "No one is touching dollar/yen. No one is interested," said a dealer at Mizuho Corporate Bank. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030509dz59004bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
