\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Higher Against Yen, Euro, But Slips vs. Pound \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2003\par 00:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar concluded a bruising week by paring some of its losses against its major rivals as traders paused to book profits. \par \par \uc2  After breaching new four-year lows against the euro early, the dollar on Friday managed to end the session slightly higher. \par \par \uc2  A rally for U.S. equities, coupled with a lack of significant U.S. economic data, provided traders with a convenient opportunity to sell euros as they positioned themselves ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "There's nothing fundamental going on here," said David Leaver, trader at Gain Capital in Warren, N.J. "It's profit-taking ... and just people squaring ahead of the weekend." \par \par \uc2  Still, the long-term uptrend in the euro appears as strong as ever after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sanctioned its strong-euro policy Thursday, noting a strong euro would help keep a lid on inflation. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen pretty violent moves in the euro and it may be time for a pullback," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Financial Corp. in London. "But the euro could go as low as $1.1150 without doing any damage to the uptrend." \par \par \uc2  The common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit a fresh four-year high against the yen at 135.30 yen in early London trading. There was a sudden slide in the yen that traders associated with Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely believed to have bought dollars and euros on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past couple of days, as the dollar-yen rate threatened to break below the key 116-yen mark. \par \par \uc2  Only at the end of the month will the Japanese government confirm whether it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the first quarter, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent 2.387 trillion yen in 24 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to cap the yen's rise against the dollar and the euro. A strong yen hurts Japanese exporters' competitiveness and economic prospects, hence the government's concern. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was trading at 117.23 yen, up from 116.85 yen late Thursday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1493, down from its global-session high of $1.1533 and down slightly from $1.1506 late Thursday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3137 francs, up from 1.3122 francs late Thursday. Sterling was at $1.6038, up from $1.6013. \par \par \uc2  Strategists said that much of the week's volatile market activity stemmed from the Federal Reserve's warning on Tuesday that falling prices could hinder economic growth, and the decision by the Federal Open Market Committee to keep already-low U.S. interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the Fed brought deflation front and center brings a whole range of new questions about Fed policy," said Ram Bhagavataula, chief economist with the Royal Bank of Scotland. "What you've seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is the extension of that: U.S. interest rates are unattractive and will continue to be so." \par \par \uc2  Though ECB President Wim Duisenberg stated on Thursday that there was nothing "excessive" about the euro's current level, French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin said Friday that the euro's current strength against the dollar poses an "important problem" for the euro-zone economy, and the ECB should consider economic and employment growth in its rate decisions. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the pound remained weak, with disappointing March United Kingdom manufacturing data doing little to help. \par \par \uc2  --Agnes Crane in New York and Adriana Arai in London contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-09-03 1802ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030509dz59006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
