\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Japan MOF Rumor Pushes Dlr, Euro Higher Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Soble \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 07:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The euro climbed to an all-time high against the yen in Asia Friday and the dollar extended its overnight rebound, as speculation that Japan's Finance Ministry is digging in to fight a surge in the yen weighed on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro climbed as high as Y135.26 in late afternoon trade, beating the peak of Y135.16 set Jan. 4, 1999, the day the single unit made its debut in the market. \par \par \uc2  "People have been watching the euro climb against the dollar, and it looks like MOF is putting a floor under the dollar/yen, so that makes the euro/yen an obvious buy," said Shuji Takano, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Aozora bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro surged against the dollar overnight after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged and signaled it wasn't concerned with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. It added to those gains in Asia, carving out a new four-year high around $1.1533 in morning trade in addition to setting new records against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese unit was weighed down across the board by speculation that the dollar's rebound from an overnight low around Y116.03 was sparked by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, which dumped nearly Y2.4 trillion into the market in the first quarter to curb the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  "People are pretty sure the MOF's been in the market," Aozora's Takano said. The ministry hasn't disclosed any of its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year until days or weeks after the fact, when data released by the Bank of Japan have tipped its hand. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar would likely test resistance in the mid-Y117 zone through global trade Friday. If the rebound's momentum proves weak, the greenback could test Y116 again next week, several traders said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y117.34, above Y116.85 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y116.67 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1517, higher than $1.1506 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.1317 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y135.17, up from Y134.45 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y132.04 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6025, up from $1.6013 in New York and higher than $1.5953 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Talk of overnight MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  focused on two major Japanese banks, which dealers said helped halt the dollar's slide by placing heavy bids near the overnight low of Y116.01. \par \par \uc2  The banks placed more bids just above Y117 Friday, several traders said, prompting speculation that the ministry was helping the dollar extend its rebound. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa sidestepped questions over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, but nonetheless made clear he prefers a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think the yen is too strong now," he said. "What I can say is that we're not disinterested." \par \par \uc2  His deputy, Vice Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, reinforced the tough message to markets. \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when there are excessive moves," he said, repeating the ministry's general stance. \par \par \uc2  The MOF revealed recently that it took a breather from yen-selling in April, when U.S. military success in Iraq kept the dollar near Y120 for most of the month. \par \par \uc2  The ministry's apparent return to action has spooked dollar bears, but traders said a sustained surge in the greenback against the yen is unlikely anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  "The market is hesitating to attack," said the head dealer at a foreign brokerage in Tokyo, "but dollar weakness is so strong that no one wants to buy, either." \par \par \uc2  -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Alastair Gale \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030509dz59000aq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
