\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro nervous ahead of ECB, yen holds recent gains. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Burton Frierson \par \uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 11:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 8 (Reuters) - The euro stood about a cent below recent four-year peaks against the dollar in nervous trade ahead of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate decision on Thursday, while the yen kept recent gains against the euro and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although 52 of 61 economists polled by Reuters last week expected no rate cut at the ECB's policy-setting meeting, speculation is rising the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut rates to spur growth in the region and halt the euro's export-harming rise. \par \par \uc2  A bounce in the yen has also raised speculation Japanese monetary authorities could once more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to protect their own export markets. Fresh comments from Finance Ministry officials reinforced this fear. \par \par \uc2  "The yen has passed trigger levels you normally see (around \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ).... Against this background I would say they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist West LB. \par \par \uc2  "I think the ECB wants to prepare the market a little more (before making a rate cut).... But, certainly the risks of a move have increased and I would put the probability for a cut at somewhere between 35 and 40 percent." \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.1350 at 0945 GMT, compared with four-year highs of $1.1453 set on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell more than 1.5 percent against the yen on Wednesday and was trading at one-week lows just below 132 on Thursday, compared with Tuesday's four-year highs of 134.48. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has leapt by nine percent against the dollar in the past six weeks, as investors flocked to higher-yielding assets. The ECB's benchmark rate of 2.50 percent compares with the U.S. federal funds rate of 1.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's decision is due at 1145 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was weak amid expectations of a rate cut by the Bank of England, which decides on rates at 1100 GMT. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll found that 20 out of 32 economists expected the bank to cut rates again three months after it chopped the benchmark rate to a 48-year low of 3.75 percent. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  NERVES \par \par \uc2  The market remained on edge over possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 116.37 yen, compared with 10-month lows just above 116.00 yen set in New York trading. \par \par \uc2  "We had some profit-taking in euro/yen yesterday which hit dollar/yen, and the key now is the Japanese authorities - I think they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again at some stage," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  During the past three years, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  every time the dollar fell towards 115 yen. In the January-March quarter, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  well before the dollar closed in on 115 yen, acting when it fell to around 117. \par \par \uc2  Data released on Thursday showed Japanese authorities spent 2.38 trillion yen ($20.44 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the January-March quarter, buying the dollar and euro. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Finance Ministry officials added to market fears. Japan's top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi said moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the past one to two weeks had been rapid, warning that officials would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they saw the need. \par \par \uc2  The MOF's Hiroshi Watanabe said in European trade that he would take action on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed, and recent moves had been extremely rapid. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030508dz5800jgb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
