\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-Yen eases vs dlr, but still weighs on Tokyo stocks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Baum \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 08:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 8 (Reuters) - The yen eased off a 10-month high against the dollar on Thursday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned it was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against rapid market moves, but the yen's strength still hurt Tokyo stocks on a weak day for Asian shares. \par \par \uc2  The warning from Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, came after the dollar sank in the wake of a Federal Reserve statement on Tuesday that flagged the risks of deflation and revived speculation it might cut rates. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi's comment that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in the past one to two weeks had been rapid and that officials would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they saw the need helped lift the dollar to around 116.60 yen  by 0600 GMT, after it fell as low as 116.07 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There's a possibility the authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in large amounts this time with an official announcement to stop the yen from rising towards 115," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  The firmer yen depressed Japanese stocks by threatening to eat into exporter's earnings. After closing at a one-month high on Wednesday, the Nikkei average  fell for the first time in six sessions to end down one percent at 8,031.55. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are concerned that the firmer yen will hurt earnings in the auto and tech sector. So they are taking some profits on those stocks here," said Koichi Kawata, deputy head of equities at SMBC Friend Securities. \par \par \uc2  Canon Inc (7751.T), the world's top maker of office machines, shed three percent. \par \par \uc2  GRINDING RECOVERY \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, investors appeared to have decided the Fed's concern over the U.S. economy was on balance bad for stocks, after Wall Street reversed Tuesday's gains on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The pieces are in place for a U.S. economic recovery, but it's going to be a grind," said Hans Kunnen, head of investment markets research at Colonial First State Investment Managers in Australia, where the benchmark index  fell 0.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan had the worst performing stock market, with the benchmark index dropping two percent on declines in U.S. technology stocks and a rise in the island's cases of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS). \par \par \uc2  Singapore, however, celebrated signs there that SARS was under control by driving up the main index  one percent by lunchtime. \par \par \uc2  "People have been staying away from the market because of the uncertainty about the impact of SARS and how long it will last. But now it seems that in Singapore, at the very least, things are becoming better," said Say Ping Low, analyst and fund manager at APS Asset Management, which manages S$900 million. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong and South Korea were closed for holidays. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, the Dow Jones Industrial Average  ended down a third of a percent at 8,560.63. The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index    finished 1.1 percent lower at 1,506.76, hit by a discouraging outlook from tech bellwether Cisco Systems Inc (CSCO.O). \par \par \uc2  Oil was steady after jumping on Wednesday when U.S. government data showed a surprise drop in crude oil inventories. \par \par \uc2  U.S. light crude  stood at $26.17 a barrel, down six cents from the close in New York, where it jumped 51 cents. \par \par \uc2  Gold  was a touch weaker at $341.25 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar was under pressure against the yen, it won some breathing space versus the euro as speculation grew that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut interest rates later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.1330, little changed from late U.S. trade but below four-year highs struck around $1.1453 earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030508dz58004xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
