\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Breaks Above $1.15; Dollar Climbs Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 23:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The euro surged across the board Thursday, in a stunning reversal of day-earlier losses after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged and signaled it wasn't concerned with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  On another day of volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the euro continued to confound its critics, ending the New York day above $1.15, after hitting another four-year high of $1.1506. The euro last traded at these levels in early January 1999, not long after its introduction. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reached an all-time peak versus sterling and is holding close to a four-year high against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar meanwhile, continued to struggle against many of its other key rivals, although it did manage to end the day appreciably higher against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the euro was trading at $1.1506, up sharply from $1.1358 late Wednesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3122 francs, down from 1.3227 francs late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.6013, up compared with $1.5962 Wednesday. The dollar was trading at 116.85 yen, up from 116.40 yen late Wednesday, and appreciably higher than its ten-month intraday low just above 116 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall was initially sparked by the Bank of England and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions to leave key interest rates unchanged at 3.75% and 2.50%, respectively. The selloff was further helped along by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's statement that there was nothing "excessive" about the euro's current level. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been expected to keep policy on hold, after recent remarks by officials suggested no imminent change. However, a worsening in various economic indicators, including business sentiment, manufacturing activity and German unemployment - along with a surging euro - kept open the possibility of a surprise rate cut. In any case, traders took the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move as a green light to push the rampaging euro higher. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB, by not cutting rates, was basically saying the euro's rise is not a cause for concern," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. "Any pullbacks will likely be shallow now, and the euro will continue to trade above the $1.1360 level against the dollar." \par \par \uc2  After surging across the board in trading on Wednesday, the yen succeeded in pushing the dollar down to a ten-month low of 116.03 yen early in the New York day, which prompted many market professionals to watch closely for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly stated its policy against over-appreciation of the yen, and traders confirmed Japanese banks had been buying dollars around the 116.05-yen level earlier Thursday, which ultimately helped the dollar to bounce back. However, traders said there was no indication of the Bank of Japan acting through accounts with the Federal Reserve Bank of New York or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the past three years, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has undertaken direct or covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar threatened to drop into the 115.50-to-115-yen range. Traders said the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would become acute if the dollar dips below 115.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  Much of the volatile market activity continues to stem from the Federal Reserve's warning on Tuesday that falling prices could hinder economic growth. \par \par \uc2  "There is a nonnegligible risk that the dollar is on the cusp of a more worrisome fall," said Jim McCormick, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Lehman Brothers in New York. "Along with the usual duo of a huge current account deficit and super-low rates, the Fed's new focus on risks from falling inflation perhaps points to a more overtly negative dollar policy." \par \par \uc2  He said it is possible the dollar may be lagging an otherwise improving picture for the U.S. economy. But he added that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are seldom behind the curve. "Perhaps the question markets should be asking is what \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are seeing that equity markets are not," Mr. McCormick said. \par \par \uc2  On the back of the weak dollar, gold futures managed to rally to eight-week highs of $349 per ounce Thursday, spurred largely by continuing speculative and broker buying. \par \par \uc2  U.S. initial jobless claim numbers released early Thursday gave little comfort to the dollar. The number of U.S. workers filing first-time applications for unemployment benefits dropped last week for a second consecutive week, although the four-week moving average, which smoothes out weekly fluctuations, rose to its highest level in more than a year. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par 