\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE: Euro Surges Again, Attempts To Break Above $1.15 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 21:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The euro continued its advance across the board Thursday afternoon in a stunning reversal of day-earlier losses in the wake of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to leave interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached a fresh four-year high against the dollar, an all-time peak versus sterling and is holding close to a four-year high against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The ECB maintained its benchmark rate at 2.50%, while the corresponding rate in the U.S. is 1.25%. The Federal Open Market Committee held rates steady on Tuesday, but put downside pressure on the dollar by calling attention to the risk of deflation. \par \par \uc2  "The yield differential has been in the market for quite a while," said Lawrence Ellenberg, Vice President of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sales with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "Considering the dollar's trading pattern over the last few months, I don't think the euro is finished, I think its got further to go." \par \par \uc2  The euro gained momentum after ECB President Wim Duisenberg said that there was nothing "excessive" about the euro's current level. \par \par \uc2  According to David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital  Trading in Warren, New Jersey, the ECB rate  decision wasn't "too surprising, but for the most part, as after the FOMC statement, the mindset was to sell the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Except against the yen, the dollar is weaker from day-earlier levels versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,  hitting a fresh four-and-a-half-year bottom versus the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Analysts had noted barriers against the euro at $1.1500, but an options-related defense began to look fragile by mid-afternoon. Around 3.15 p.m. EDT (1915 GMT), the euro was trading at 1.1491 after momentarily touching a session high just above $1.1500. The euro is up sharply from $1.1358 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar is at Y116.93, up from Y116.40 late Wednesday in New York, but appreciably higher than its session low just above Y116.00. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3135, down from CHF1.3227 late Wednesday but above the day's low at CHF1.3089. Sterling was at $1.6003, up compared with $1.5962 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide has hiked volatilities across the board in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while sparking a shakeout in many other exchange rate pairs also. The euro traded at its highest level ever against the U.K. pound, touching GBP0.7180, to replace the previous peak stretching back to Jan. 5, 1999, just a few days after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's introduction. \par \par \uc2  With the euro on such a rampage, analysts believe it's also possible the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might manage to sustainably break  resistance around the $1.15 line. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen, which surged across the board in trading Wednesday while the euro sold off, succeeded in pushing the dollar down to a ten-month low just above Y116.03 early Thursday, prompting many market professionals to watch closely for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly stated its policy against over-appreciation of the yen, and traders confirmed Japanese banks had been buying dollars around the Y116.05 level earlier Thursday. However, traders said there was no indication of the Bank of Japan acting through accounts with the Federal Reserve Bank of New York or the ECB. \par \par \uc2  In the past three years, the BOJ has undertaken direct or covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar threatened to drop into the Y115.50-to-115.00 range. Traders said the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ would become acute if the dollar dips below Y115.80. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires,201-938-2248; \par \par \uc2  michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Robert Flint contributed to this story) \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030508dz580019e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
