\par\par \b \uc2  After Early Dip, Traders Flag Some Obstacles To Dlr Fall \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 18:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- After sinking to multiyear and multimonth lows against its rivals Thursday morning, the dollar has managed to bounce back from its worst levels of the day. \par \par \uc2  While slightly ahead of levels late Wednesday versus the yen, the dollar is still down on the day versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having hit fresh four-year low against the euro and a four-and-a-half-year bottom versus the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are now cautioning that there are significant, though not insurmountable, barriers that could slow a further immediate decline. \par \par \uc2  Traders say a substantial number of positions have been established on the expectation that the euro will not break above $1.1500 and the yen will not fall below Y116.00. \par \par \uc2  "I think the barriers are firm but not unbreakable, and a lot of intraday positions have been set on the thinking that these levels will not be crossed," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital Trading in Warren, New Jersey. \par \par \uc2  Around midday, the dollar was trading at Y116.60, up a touch from Y116.40 late Wednesday in New York, but appreciably higher than its session low just above Y116.00. The euro had also consolidated somewhat at $1.1470, up sharply from $1.1358 late Wednesday but off session highs of $1.1484. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3152, down from CHF1.3227 late Wednesday but above the day's low at CHF1.3089. Sterling was at $1.6001, up compared with $1.5962 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's surge against the dollar, sterling and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  came the back of dual decisions by the Bank of England and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave key interest rates unchanged at 3.75% and 2.50%, respectively. The euro peaked after a statement by ECB President Wim Duisenberg that there was nothing excessive about the euro's current level. The move came after the Federal Open Market Committee also held rates steady on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The rate decisions weren't too surprising, but for the most part, as after the FOMC statement, the mindset was to sell the dollar," said Leaver. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide has hiked volatilities across the board in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while sparking a shakeout in many other exchange rate pairs also. The euro traded at its highest level ever against the U.K. pound, touching GBP0.7166, to replace the previous peak stretching back to Jan. 5, 1999, just a few days after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's introduction. With the euro on such a rampage, analysts believe it's also possible the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might manage to break through resistance under the $1.15 level. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen, which surged across the board in trading Wednesday while the euro sold off, succeeded in pushing the dollar down to a ten-month low just above Y116.03 early Thursday, prompting many market professionals to watch closely for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly stated its policy against over-appreciation of the yen, and traders confirmed Japanese banks had been buying dollars around the Y116.05 level earlier Thursday. However, traders said there was no indication of the Bank of Japan acting through accounts with the Federal Reserve Bank of New York or the ECB. \par \par \uc2  In the past three years, the BOJ has undertaken direct or covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar threatened to drop into the Y115.50-to-115.00 range. Traders said the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ would become acute if the dollar dips below Y115.80. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, \par \par \uc2  201-938-2248, email:michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Robert Flint contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030508dz580014e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
