\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Vols Higher, But Dollar Off Session Lows \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 18:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were higher Thursday in New York, as the dollar's decline against both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  moderated mid-morning after commencing at a rapid pace early Thursday. \par \par \uc2  During the morning plunge, which occurred after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted to leave key interest rates unchanged at 2.50%, the dollar sank to a four-year low against the euro, with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbing to $1.1484, a move that was also helped along by a statement by ECB President Wim Duisenberg that there was nothing excessive about the euro's current level. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England also left in place its interest rate of 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  "The rate decisions weren't too surprising, but for the most part, the mindset was to sell the dollar," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital Trading in Warren, N.J. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which surged across the board in trading on Wednesday, afterwards succeeded in pushing the dollar down to a 10-month low of Y116.03 early Thursday, which has prompted many market professionals to watch closely for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly stated its policy against over-appreciation of the yen, and at mid-morning Thursday traders confirmed the buying of dollars by Japanese banks around the Y116.05 level. \par \par \uc2  Traders say a substantial number of intraday positions have been set up under the expectation that the euro will not break above $1.1500 and the yen will not fall below Y116.00. \par \par \uc2  At about 12 p.m. EDT, the dollar was trading at Y116.59, up from Y116.40 late Wednesday in New York, but appreciably higher from its session low of Y116.03. The euro had also consolidated somewhat, and was trading at $1.1462, up sharply from $1.1358 late Wednesday but off session highs of $1.1484 immediately following the ECB statement. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3157, down from CHF1.3227 late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.6039, up compared with $1.5962 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.80%-10.10%, up from 9.40%-9.65% Wednesday in New York. One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 10.50%-10.90%, up from 10.50%-10.75%, earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor dollar puts at -1.40% to -1.10%, compared with 1.00%-1.30% in Tokyo on Tuesday. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls are favored against the dollar by 0.60%-1.10%. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248; michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030508dz580014c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
