\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Warns Of More  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  After 1Q Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Twaronite \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 10:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned Thursday it would act as necessary to stem the rising yen, after it released data which clearly showed the extent of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of 2003. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent Y2.387 trillion in 24 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from January to March in an effort to cap the yen's rise against the dollar and the euro, the data showed. \par \par \uc2  The actual amount wasn't a surprise, as Bank of Japan current account data already revealed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly yen-selling on the ministry's behalf. \par \par \uc2  But the data offered new details on the timing of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Market participants examined the statistics for clues as to when financial authorities could next back up their verbal warnings with action. \par \par \uc2  Such clues are timely, as the dollar is now trading just above a 10-month low of Y116.07 hit overnight. At 0840 GMT, the dollar was at Y116.37. \par \par \uc2  "Verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a certainty from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Morgan Stanley's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist, Stephen Jen. \par \par \uc2  "However, the question is whether they will be able to support a falling dollar and whether the U.S. and Euroland will continue to allow Japan to run such a competitive devaluation policy, when they themselves face risks of deflation." \par \par \uc2  Japan has consistently denied it is seeking to manipulate the yen lower, and has referred to its yen-selling as market "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  But it's no secret that financial authorities here fear a strong yen would make Japanese exports less competitive in overseas markets and add to deflationary pressure at home. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Hold Fire For Now\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Finance Ministry official briefing reporters on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data said the ministry is prepared to take action against\par the yen's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves are excessive and inappropriate. We're\par ready to act against that," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par His comments were soon echoed by Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance\par minister for international affairs, who said Japan "will take\par appropriate steps as needed."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the details of when past \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was timed, revealed in\par Thursday's data, suggest that the ministry has reason to hold its fire\par for now.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's yen-weakening efforts were most effective when the market was\par significantly dollar-short, and "the market's not particularly short at\par the moment - we don't seem to have that large overhang yet," said J.P.\par Morgan strategist James Malcolm.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Positions notwithstanding, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "risks get higher the lower\par down we go," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A rising yen, though, is a source of worry for many companies in\par Japan, including the country's beleaguered life insurance firms. Not\par only does it wipe out profits on the overseas assets they left unhedged,\par but if the dollar trades below Y116, it begins to erode gains on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -linked speculative bets some of the larger companies made,\par analysts say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The perception of systemic risk for the financial sector would get\par much larger" if insurers were to lose these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -linked gains, said\par J.P. Morgan's Malcolm.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To curb speculative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves in the first quarter,\par Japanese authorities sold Y2.291 trillion to buy dollars and Y95.6\par billion to buy euros.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In January, Japan sold Y678.1 billion for dollars. In February, it\par sold Y1.061 trillion for dollars and Y81 billion for euros.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In March, it sold Y551.8 billion for dollars and Y14.6 billion for\par euros.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com (Ron Harui also contributed to this story)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Bennett Richardson\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030508dz58000lm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
