\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen surges to 10-month high vs dollar - euro stalls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2003\par 21:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 7 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a 10-month low versus the yen on Wednesday in a rapid sell-off that tested Japan's resolve to protect its vital export sector by keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak against the greenback and the euro. \par \par \uc2  "I think the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping the dollar above 116 yen at this point," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York. \par \par \uc2  In January and February, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling yen for dollars, catching many investors off guard. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday traders said they could not detect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to stop the sharp dollar decline, which makes Japan's exports less competitive on the world market. \par \par \uc2  "We do think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks are significant given the pace of the dollar/yen fall," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  She said the lack of comments from Japanese policymakers on the yen's strength has emboldened shorter-term investors to sell the dollar. \par \par \uc2  At the same time comments this week from U.S. officials supporting a strong dollar policy did little to slow the dollar sell-off, and some analysts were calling them hollow. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 116.10 yen  on Wednesday before trimming its losses to trade at 116.41 yen, a loss of 0.85 percent from Tuesday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also affected by the silence surrounding the yen's strength, falling 1.66 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade at 132.20 yen . \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR REBOUNDS ON EURO \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose broadly versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recovering a bit from the four-year low it hit on Tuesday versus the euro after the Federal Reserve left interest rates alone but gave a weak assessment of the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  A weak outlook signaled U.S. interest rates are likely to stay low or drop more from their current level of 1.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  With European interest rates at 2.50 percent, investors are trading in their dollars for euro and shifting cash into the euro zone in search of higher returns on their money. \par \par \uc2  Investors have ignored the fact that relative economic growth rates in the United States outstrip those found in the euro zone, choosing to follow trends rather than fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But the euro's nearly 8 percent surge against the dollar in the last month is raising concerns it has moved too far too fast, especially because the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is meeting on Thursday to discuss rates in the sluggish euro zone. \par \par \uc2  While a Reuters poll shows economists don't expect a rate cut, investors exercised caution by selling the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is hesitant to rise against the dollar because of a possible rate cut by the ECB. Our base-case is for no rate cut, but the risks for a cut a little greater than a week or two ago," said Brickman. \par \par \uc2  The euro traded at $1.1364 , off 0.66 percent. \par \par \uc2  The White House reiterated on Wednesday what U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said on Tuesday, that he backs a strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  "This is the minimum lip service one can pay to strong dollar policy. It is one step above not saying anything at all," said Steven Englander, chief North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell against the dollar a day before the Bank of England takes a decision on interest rates, currently at 3.75 percent. Sterling traded at $1.5980 , off 1.13 percent. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll found economists about split on the likelihood of a BoE rate cut on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030507dz57011z4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
