\par\par \b \uc2  Yen Rallies Against Major  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2003\par 00:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen jumped to the top of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heap Wednesday, with\par sterling ending at the bottom of the heap in volatile trading ahead of\par major European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meetings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was trading at 116.40 yen, down from 117.20 yen late Tuesday in New York, but off from lows of &#165;116.15 earlier in the day. The euro was trading at $1.1358, down sharply from $1.1438 late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.5962, down steeply compared with $1.6138 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen's surge against the pound was particularly sharp, with the pound plummeting to an intraday low at 185.17 yen from a high at 190.16 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar paused in its fall against the euro and regained some ground against the pound, as the U.K. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply across the board on fears the Bank of England may cut interest rates at its Thursday meeting. \par \par \uc2  "We saw some significant moves today, but given the volatility we've seen with world events over the last month or so, the moves were fairly in-tune with what we've been seeing," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank and Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Open Market Committee's warning Tuesday that deflation could hinder economic growth continued to cast a shadow over markets, underscoring the dollar's fragility but also providing a convenient opportunity to book profits. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed statement accelerated the moves [Tuesday], and now that the sentiment seems to be so dollar-negative, people thought it was time to move on and we saw a lot of profit-taking with the euro," said Mr. Mazanec. \par \par \uc2  As traders adjusted positions, the yen seized the opportunity to play catch up with the dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after lagging behind during earlier waves of dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  "It took the market a bit of time to examine the FOMC statement," said Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York, referring to the spiking and retreating nature of Wednesday's trades. "Now it's really clear that U.S. rates will be low for the foreseeable future, and there's less hope for growth. It's taken things down a notch." \par \par \uc2  Most strategists saw the dollar's paring back of losses against the euro as an inevitable correction of Tuesday's spike, which saw the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit four-year highs against the dollar at $1.1451, its best level since January 1999. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's moves "were so exaggerated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are coming back to shore," said Ram Bhagavataula, chief economist with Royal Bank of Scotland in New York. "Given everything the market knows about the economy, the dollar's decline is close to coming to an end." \par \par \uc2  Besides the yen, sterling also took major hits against the euro, which reached a new four-year high at 71.40  pence. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the Bank of England, unlike the U.S. Federal Reserve, is notoriously difficult to read before decisions are announced, which helps explain the skittishness of investors to hold long sterling positions. \par \par \uc2  "I will be extremely surprised if they cut rates," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research with Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh, since the recent weakening of the pound has already served as a de facto easing that should boost exports. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also meets Thursday, is widely expected to stand pat on interest rates, although the consensus on June is for a modest quarter-point easing. \par \par \uc2  Of 22 economists replying to a survey by Dow Jones Newswires, only two expected the ECB Governing Council to trim the 2.5% minimum bid rate. Higher-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro has proven attractive as investors seek higher returns. \par \par \uc2  The pound's decline coincided with across-the-board gains for the yen. The yen has gained fairly consistently in recent days, raising the specter of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with 115.80 yen and 115.50 yen being mentioned as important levels in its rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Over the last few days the yen has been strengthening consistently," said Nick Bennenbroek, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "But there's no specific news, it just appears to be market-driven." \par \par \uc2  Jawboning from Japanese officials has apparently worn thin. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to hinder the yen's appreciation and fears of BOJ action have served to cap the yen's advance. \par \par \uc2  Write to Michael Deibert at michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  05-07-03 1859ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030507dz5700a15\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppi