\par\par \b \uc2  Sterling Drops On BOE Rate Fears As Yen Makes Big Jump \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2003\par 19:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Sterling fell precipitously against its rivals Wednesday on fears the Bank of England may cut interest rates at its meeting Thursday, while the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zoomed ahead versus most rivals. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell a dramatic five figures against the yen, bottoming out at Y185.17, a two-month low,  from a session high of Y190.04. The British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also took major hits against the euro, which reached a new four-year high at 0.7140 U.K. pence, and the usually-battered dollar, which touched its highest level against the pound since April 29th at $1.5911. \par \par \uc2  Around 1.30 p.m. EDT (1730 GMT), the U.K. pound was at Y185.90, with the euro at 0.7110 pence. \par \par \uc2  Sterling's decline coincided with cross-the-board gains for the yen, which had until the beginning of the week, lagged behind other widely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in advancing on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that the Bank of England, unlike the U.S. Federal Reserve, is notoriously difficult to read before  decisions are announced, which helps explain the skittishness of investors to hold long sterling positions. \par \par \uc2  "I will be extremely surprised if they cut rates," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research with  Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh, since the recent weakening of the pound has already served as a de facto easing that should boost exports. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also meets Thursday, is widely expected to stand pat on interest rates, although the consensus on June is for a modest 25-basis-point easing. \par \par \uc2  Of 22 economists replying to a survey by Dow Jones Newswires, only two expected the ECB Governing Council to trim the 2.50% minimum bid rate. Higher-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro has proven attractive as investors seek higher returns. \par \par \uc2  Sterling's fall is also linked to forces driving others \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Following its sharp fall in the wake of the Fed's warning that deflation could hinder economic growth, some analysts are seeing the retracement of some of the dollar's stark losses in recent days as all but inevitable as trader's book profits. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar pared part of its dramatic losses against some of its rivals, succeeding in pushing the euro back below the $1.1400 range reached Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's moves were so exaggerated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are coming back to shore," said Ram Bhagavataula, chief economist with Royal Bank of Scotland in New York. "Given everything the market knows about the economy, the dollar's decline is close to coming to an end." \par \par \uc2  The yen, as well, has gained fairly consistently in recent days, raising the specter of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Y115.80 and Y115.50 being mentioned as important levels in its rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Over the last few days the yen has been strengthening consistently," says Nick Bennenbroek, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "But there's no specific news, it just appears to be market-driven." \par \par \uc2  Jawboning from Japanese officials has apparently worn thin. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to hinder the yen's appreciation and fears of BOJ action have served to cap the yen's advance. \par \par \uc2  Early afternoon Wednesday, the dollar was trading at Y116.40, down from Y117.20 late Tuesday in New York, but off  lows of Y116.15 earlier in the day. The euro was trading at $1.1350, down sharply from $1.1438 late Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3245, steady from CHF1.3245 late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.5970, down compared with $1.6138 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, \par \par \uc2  201-938-2248, email:michael.deibert@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030507dz57008hl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
