\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro leaps to 4-yr highs, powered by Fed jitters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2003\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 6 (Reuters) - The euro surged to fresh four year highs against the dollar, sterling and the yen on Tuesday, riding a wave of investor concerns about the health of the U.S. economy ahead of a U.S. Federal Reserve meeting later. \par \par \uc2  The Fed's policy setting committee will announce an interest rate decision at 1815 GMT. Few in the market expect a cut this month but are wary rates may have to fall next month, which would depress paltry returns on U.S. fixed income assets even more. \par \par \uc2  "This is a general move against the dollar," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "The market doesn't believe they will cut at this stage but if they are able to make an assessment on the economy, people expect the risks are on the downside which would point to a cut in June." \par \par \uc2  The euro charged to $1.1341 in early European trade, its highest in four years and up a third of a percent on the day. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now gained nearly eight percent on the dollar this year. \par \par \uc2  The euro climbed above 134 yen, also a four year high, while the dollar held just above last week's one-month low against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 118.38 yen and hit a two-month low against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also fell in the euro's path, hitting a four year low at 70.39 pence as Europe trade got going. \par \par \uc2  BEST OF BIG THREE \par \par \uc2  Traders said Japanese investors continued to favour the euro and other higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar to the detriment of the greenback, where the fed funds rate is at a four decade low of 1.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also cited rumours of buying by mutual funds, Middle Eastern banks and Asian central banks as supporting the euro. \par \par \uc2  "There has been steady uptrend in the euro for some time now, mainly because of the U.S. current account deficit and a search for yield," said Julian Jessop, chief European economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "The market is also looking for liquidity and there is also a rebalancing of reserves by Asian central banks so the euro tends to outperform." \par \par \uc2  Concern that Japanese authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent yen strength against the dollar has helped put a floor under the greenback around 118 yen and worked in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's favour. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is the best of the Big Three. It's not a huge desire to buy euro assets but people aren't confident buying, yen given what the Bank of Japan might do," Hayward said. \par \par \uc2  A media report last week said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would call for yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a stimulus package. The package is expected to be proposed later on Tuesday by Japan's ruling coalition parties in an effort to prop up stock prices and boost the economy. \par \par \uc2  It is likely to include calls for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy exchange-traded stock funds, or ETFs, and to buy back shares, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders will be watching for any measures designed to weaken the yen to boost exports. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030506dz5600h4y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
