\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar holding tight as market awaits fresh leads. \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2003\par 01:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     2333 - Dollar little changed against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in early Asian trade and expected to remain subdued with\par Japanese markets closed for a public holiday.\par     - It made some gains on Friday in New York on a report from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Jiji Press that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would call for yen weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  as part of an expected stimulus package due to be announced \par \par \uc2  this week. \par \par \uc2  - Grim U.S. labour force data met investors' expectations, \par \par \uc2  leaving no new direction for the U.S. dollar. Unemployment rate \par \par \uc2  rose to a four-month high of 6.0 percent as the economy shed \par \par \uc2  48,000 payrolls against forecast of a 5.9 percent jobless rate \par \par \uc2  and 53,000 loss in payrolls. \par \par \uc2  - While still bearish on the U.S. dollar, string of \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy-setting meetings this week expected to keep \par \par \uc2  investors wary, giving the greenback a reprieve after last \par \par \uc2  week's beating. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     - Federal Reserve is seen leaving its funds rate unchanged\par at 1.25 percent. Double click         to see latest Fed poll.\par     - Reserve Bank of Australia also seen keeping its official\par cash rate unchanged at 4.75 percent. Double click          for\par more.\par     - In contrast, most economists polled by Reuters expect\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par another rate cut by the Bank of England this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba000002003050